  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Intro to Loops",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Intro to Loops",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nThis lesson introduces the concept of iteration (loops) through real-world scenarios starting with the \"Paper Toss Challenge,\" where they experience the difference between repeating an action a fixed number of times versus repeating until a specific condition is met. Then, students create flowcharts to model a for loop and while loop. Finally, students utilize AI to translate their  flowcharts into text-based pseudocode.",
        "student_overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nLearn about the concept of iteration loops through real-world scenarios, as well as visual and text-based representations. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Intro to Loops",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Counted Repetition",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Counted Repetition",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do for loops control how many times we repeat?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how for loops control repeated actions by acting out loop behavior, debugging a broken password generator, and implementing a for-loop feature in an app using AI-assisted code. They learn how start, stop, and step values affect loop outcomes and how small logic mistakes lead to off-by-one errors. By the end, students can analyze loop behavior, correct boundary issues, and use AI intentionally to generate and refine functional loop-based code.",
        "student_overview": "**How do for loops control how many times we repeat?**\n\nToday you learn how for loops make apps repeat actions and how a small mistake in a loop can cause a program to run the wrong number of times. You act out a loop, fix a bug in a real app, and add a feature to an app using a for loop with help from AI. By the end, you understand how loop settings like start, stop, and step control what your program does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Counted Repetition",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Open-Ended Repetition",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Open-Ended Repetition",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nThis lesson introduces open-ended repetition using while loops through the career of a Game Engineer. Students step into the role of an engineer reviewing AI-generated game code — using a diagnostic checklist to identify and fix buggy loops, then collaborating with AI to build a working loop themselves. Along the way, they interview a Game Engineer about how loops show up in real games and what to check before trusting code from AI.\n",
        "student_overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nStep into the role of a Game Engineer to find bugs in AI-generated game code, then partner with AI to build a working game loop yourself.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Open-Ended Repetition",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Displaying Arrays as List Views",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Displaying Arrays as List Views",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do loops traverse arrays to render a basic view?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how apps use loops to present clear, trustworthy list views by adding transparency cues such as counts, filter summaries, and empty states. They then build a full read → transform → render pipeline that uses loops to filter data and intentionally control what information is shown and how it appears. By the end, students can design list views that communicate system behavior clearly and use looping logic to produce purposeful, user-centered displays.",
        "student_overview": "**How do loops traverse arrays to render a basic view?**\n\nToday you learn how apps decide what to show by adding messages that explain what's happening with the data — like how many results there are or why a list is empty. You then build your own pipeline that reads data, filters it, and shows only what users need. By the end, you understand how your app makes display decisions and how to make those decisions feel clear and trustworthy."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Displaying Arrays as List Views",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Displaying Objects as Cards",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Displaying Objects as Cards",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we render richer records reliably?**\n\nStudents learn how to turn objects from a dataset into clear, readable cards by choosing which fields to display and planning deliberate fallback values for missing data. They explore how AI handles missing values with and without field labels, sketch a card layout, and use AI Tutor to help build and refine their card before coding fallback checks themselves. The lesson connects technical decisions — which fields to show, what to display when data is null — to design choices that affect how users interpret and trust information.",
        "student_overview": "**How do we render richer records reliably?**\n\nLearn how to turn data objects into clear, readable cards by choosing key fields, planning fallback values for missing data, and recognizing how the decisions you make as a developer shape what users see and trust."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Displaying Objects as Cards",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Filtering and Transparency",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Filtering and Transparency",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can a filter prevent or reinforce biases?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the role of a Responsible AI Specialist by auditing and improving a job/opportunity matching app called OpportunityMatch. Students test the app using different user profiles, identify how filters can help some users while leaving others out, and then use AI assistance to improve the app’s filtering options. By the end of the lesson, students understand how filters, defaults, and missing controls can shape what users see, and how transparency labels can make digital systems more understandable and responsible.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Filtering and Transparency activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can a filter prevent or reinforce biases?**\n\nToday you will act as a Responsible AI Specialist. You will test a job/opportunity matching app called OpportunityMatch, use user profiles to notice who the filters help or leave out, and then improve the app so users have more control. You will also add a transparency label so users better understand why certain results appear. By the end, you will see how small design and coding choices can make a system more fair, useful, and clear."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Filtering and Transparency",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: Pure Functions vs Side Effects",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Pure Functions vs Side Effects",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What’s the difference between transforming data and causing effects?**\n\nThis lesson introduces students to the difference between functions that safely return new data and ones that accidentally change the original. Through a hands-on sticky-note activity and simple method investigations, students see how mutation happens and why copying data matters. They finish by using AI to help fix a buggy function, practicing how to check whether code is truly safe.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Pure Functions vs Side Effects activity guide (one for each student)\n- Provide a small stack of sticky notes for each student",
        "student_overview": "**What’s the difference between transforming data and causing effects?**\n\nLearn how to spot which functions safely create new data and which ones accidentally change the original, and use that understanding to fix buggy code with AI support. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: Pure Functions vs Side Effects",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Copy, Don't Mutate",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Copy, Don't Mutate",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**When should we copy data, and how do users recover data that has been modified?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore why apps need reliable Reset and Undo features and how those features depend on copying data rather than mutating it. They design a user journey showing how these actions should work, then fix a broken Reset function and build an Undo history stack using state snapshots and push/pop logic. By the end, students understand how the copy-don’t-mutate strategy preserves data integrity and enables predictable recovery behaviors in interactive systems.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Copy, Don’t Mutate activity guide (one for each student)\n- Provide a small stack of sticky notes for each student",
        "student_overview": "**When should we copy data, and how do users recover data that has been modified?**\n\nToday you learn how apps let users recover from mistakes by using safe copies of data instead of changing the original. You design how Reset and Undo should work for a real interface, fix a broken Reset button, and build an Undo feature that steps back through saved history states. By the end, you see how keeping clean copies of data helps your app stay reliable and lets users reverse actions without losing information."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Copy, Don't Mutate",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Who is surfaced or sidelined by our filters, and how can we show that?**\n\nIn this lesson, students investigate how categories and thresholds quietly shape visibility in apps and how certain voices or items disappear as filters become more restrictive. Through hands-on exploration, they examine who gets excluded and why transparency matters in user-facing tools. Students then redesign filters and labels to prioritize inclusion and visibility, using clear labels so users understand why results appear the way they do. Students see how filtering choices influence fairness and trust, and how thoughtful design prevents misleading or exclusionary outcomes.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**Who is surfaced or sidelined by our filters, and how can we show that?**\n\nExplore how filters can unintentionally hide people or items — then redesign them so users understand what they're seeing and why."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Schema Design for Simple Features",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Schema Design for Simple Features",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How should our data shape make features simpler, correct, and fair?**\n\nIn this lesson, students investigate how inconsistent field names and data types lead to misinterpretation by both humans and AI. They formally learn the term schema — connecting it to the data plans they have been designing since Unit 3 — and explore why clear, well-structured schemas are both a technical necessity and an ethical responsibility. Students refactor a flawed schema, write a loop that cleans messy records by standardizing field names and data types, and add constraint checks to handle edge cases like missing fields and unexpected values. By the end, students understand how schema decisions shape what AI can reason about, and why careful data design matters for building apps that work reliably and fairly.",
        "student_overview": "**How should our data shape make features simpler, correct, and fair?**\n\nInvestigate how inconsistent field names and data types confuse both people and AI, then fix a messy dataset by writing a cleaning loop and constraint checks that make the data reliable. Along the way, you learn the formal name for something you've been doing since Unit 3 — schema design."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Schema Design for Simple Features",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: Interactive Data Dashboard Project",
      "name": "Lesson 11: Interactive Data Dashboard Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we design an interactive dashboard that helps people find what they need while keeping algorithmic choices fair, transparent, and under user control?**\n\nThis project guides students through building a data dashboard that renders items dynamically and supports interactive filtering, sorting, and Reset/Undo behavior. Along the way, students analyze the ethical implications of their filtering logic, refine the UI for clarity and usability, and document their iterative debugging process. AI functions as a partner in reasoning and refinement, with students explaining how AI suggestions shaped their final designs.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Interactive Data Dashboard Project Rubric to students (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we design an interactive dashboard that helps people find what they need while keeping algorithmic choices fair, transparent, and under user control?**\n\nIn this project, you build an interactive data dashboard that displays information clearly and lets users explore it with filters, sorting tools, and a Reset/Undo feature. You also examine how your design choices affect fairness and visibility, and use AI to help plan, debug, and refine your work."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: Interactive Data Dashboard Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "End of Unit Assessment",
