  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "b8a302fb-e6d9-4471-b300-6f4b68322c32",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8a302fb-e6d9-4471-b300-6f4b68322c32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "64ab2f87-627c-4e2e-9a0a-e02e2ca2d5e2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2222222f-d600-4c9f-93ef-b3b316a2e328",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2222222f-d600-4c9f-93ef-b3b316a2e328",
        "lesson_activity.key": "64ab2f87-627c-4e2e-9a0a-e02e2ca2d5e2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d0437bf-c1cb-4dfd-a6d9-e1fb6da6db74",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate students' prior knowledge about repetition and surface the idea that some tasks repeat a fixed number of times while others repeat until a condition is met.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *Round 1: You shoot five free throws.*\n* *Round 2: You shoot as many times as you need to just make one shot.*\n* *Which round do you think would take longer—and why?*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface the idea that a fixed number of repetitions (like five tosses) is different from repeating until a condition is met (making a basket), and that the condition-based round can take more or fewer tosses depending on what happens.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might have different answers for which round would take longer depending on their skill level or knowledge of basketball. The important takeaway is that Round 1 will always involve 5 free throws and Round 2 will depend on how long it takes to sink a free throw.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d0437bf-c1cb-4dfd-a6d9-e1fb6da6db74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a714218a-4da2-4a83-8d18-f0c3a87bd39d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20fc55ab-07d2-42cd-994f-83317ae06fba",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20fc55ab-07d2-42cd-994f-83317ae06fba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a714218a-4da2-4a83-8d18-f0c3a87bd39d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2bb15d6-477e-41a7-89bf-c9470b80c1d7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2bb15d6-477e-41a7-89bf-c9470b80c1d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a714218a-4da2-4a83-8d18-f0c3a87bd39d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6076e48-8998-46fd-9743-ecc1cd018149",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**Help students notice that different prompt wording can lead AI Tutor to generate different loop patterns.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1. Explain that they will prompt AI Tutor, compare two loop outputs, and record what changed between them.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Loops and AI",
        "progression_name": "Explore Two Loop Patterns",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students do not need to know the names of the loop types yet. Focus the discussion on what changed in the prompts and outputs, then use their observations to introduce the difference between count-controlled and condition-controlled loops.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6076e48-8998-46fd-9743-ecc1cd018149",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94578571-c5d9-46c3-a715-1f6ff289a126",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pull 2–3 student examples and display them side by side. Choose examples where the prompt wording led to different loop outputs, such as a prompt with “for each” and a prompt with “keep checking until.”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What patterns do you notice between the prompts and the loops AI Tutor generated?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students notice that AI Tutor responded to the exact language of the prompt. Prompts that describe a known count or each item tend to produce one loop pattern, while prompts that describe repeating until something happens tend to produce another. Students do not need to name the loop types yet — focus on what they observe.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Avoid naming the loop types too quickly. Let students first describe what they notice, then connect their observations to count-controlled and condition-controlled loops on the next slide.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94578571-c5d9-46c3-a715-1f6ff289a126",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76c05e17-702d-4631-93a6-bbd19f5c984f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use this slide to explain why AI Tutor chose each loop type. AI does not interpret what a student means — it matches the language of the prompt to common code patterns. Prompts with a fixed count or number tend to produce a for loop; prompts with condition-based language like \"until\" or \"keep checking\" tend to produce a while loop. Refer back to the student examples from the discussion. Highlight prompts that used fixed-count wording, such as \"five free throws,\" and prompts that used condition wording, such as \"until they make a basket.\" Ask students how the loop's stopping rule changed between the two. Listen for students connecting the prompt wording to the type of loop generated.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that AI Tutor is not deciding what the student meant. It is matching prompt language to common code patterns. Responsible programmers check whether the generated loop stops in the right way: after a set count, or when a condition changes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76c05e17-702d-4631-93a6-bbd19f5c984f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15331d32-c255-464e-afaf-31cdea024266",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Build students' understanding of iteration, for loops, and while loops by connecting vocabulary and definitions to annotated flowchart examples.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Iterating with AI Unit Guide.\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Making Sense of Loops"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15331d32-c255-464e-afaf-31cdea024266",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83298db3-056d-46dd-8ee3-646665f2c133",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define iteration. Explain that iteration means doing the same set of steps over and over again — not just repeating an action once, but cycling through a complete set of steps multiple times. Use the laundry example on the slide: each load involves the same four steps (wash, dry, fold, put away), and doing all four steps once is one iteration. Point out that in programming, loops are how we write iteration in code. Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What are some examples of tasks you repeat, and how do you know when to stop?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that many real-world tasks involve repeating the same steps, and that there is usually some signal or condition that tells us when we are finished. Listen for students using the word \"iteration\" to describe their example and identifying what counts as one complete cycle of steps.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to think of an example, offer a few starting points such as brushing teeth, making a sandwich, or running laps. Encourage them to identify what one full iteration looks like in their example.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83298db3-056d-46dd-8ee3-646665f2c133",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac21796b-1781-40c3-b9e4-99abd3cab602",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define for loop. Explain that a for loop is used when you know ahead of time exactly how many times you need to repeat something — the count is set before the loop begins. Connect back to the warm up: Round 1 (shooting five free throws) is a for loop because the number of repetitions is fixed. Point out that \"count-controlled\" is another name for this type of loop. Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce start value, stop value, and step as the three parts that control how many times a for loop runs. Use the staircase on the slide as a concrete example: the bottom is the start value (0), the top is the stop value (6), and each step up is the step (1). Emphasize that every for loop has all three parts, and that changing any one of them changes how the loop behaves. Let students know they will identify these parts in a for loop flowchart on the next slide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to reveal each label on the for loop flowchart one at a time. As each label appears, briefly explain what it represents: the start value sets the counter's beginning, the stop value sets when the loop ends, the stop value check is where the loop compares the counter to the stop value each time, and the step is what changes the counter. Direct students to annotate the flowchart in their Unit Guide as each label is revealed.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac21796b-1781-40c3-b9e4-99abd3cab602",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1c17ff1-6fc5-473c-8dca-9b64d72f16be",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define while loop. Explain that a while loop is used when you do not know ahead of time how many times something needs to repeat — the loop keeps going as long as a condition remains true, and stops only when that condition changes. Connect back to the warm up: Round 2 (shooting until you make one basket) is a while loop because the number of repetitions depends on what happens, not a fixed count. Point out that \"condition-controlled\" is another name for this type of loop. Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that unlike a for loop, a while loop does not have a fixed count — it only has a start value and a stop condition. Use the traffic light on the slide as an example: the car starts moving when the light is green (start value) and stops when the light turns red (stop condition). The number of seconds the car drives is not fixed — it depends entirely on when the condition changes. \n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To contrast with the for loop, ask students: \"Could you say exactly how many times this loop runs? Why or why not?\" Students should recognize that unlike the pushup for loop — where the count was always 10 — the traffic light loop could stop at any time depending on when the condition changes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1c17ff1-6fc5-473c-8dca-9b64d72f16be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd68ab41-df69-46cb-a3b4-a46f505b48a0",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to reveal the start value and stop condition for the while loop flowchart. As each label appears, explain what it represents: the start value sets the initial state of the condition variable (makeBasket = false), and the stop condition is where the loop checks whether to keep going or stop. Point out that this is the same free throw scenario from the warm up — the loop keeps running until the player makes a basket, just like Round 2. Direct students to annotate the flowchart in their Unit Guide as each label is revealed.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd68ab41-df69-46cb-a3b4-a46f505b48a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd24d9be-3a6a-4b39-aeb7-260d52149a75",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd24d9be-3a6a-4b39-aeb7-260d52149a75",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "823ba1ea-0676-4e21-8b42-568f61eebc7e",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students the opportunity to apply their understanding of for loops and while loops by designing their own flowcharts, using AI to generate pseudocode, and checking their understanding of both loop types.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 2 to complete Levels 2-5. \n* **Level 2:** Students design their own for loop flowchart representing a personally relevant scenario, labeling start value, stop value, and step.\n* **Level 3:** Students design their own while loop flowchart representing a scenario where repetition depends on a condition, labeling start value and stop condition.\n* **Level 4:** Students use AI to turn one of their flowcharts into pseudocode, then edit the output to accurately match their diagram.\n* **Level 5:** Students complete a Check for Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  For Levels 2 and 3, check that flowcharts include the correct labels for each loop type — start value, stop value, and step for for loops; start value and stop condition for while loops. Look for scenarios that are clearly count-controlled or condition-controlled and redirect any that blur the distinction. For Level 4, check that students are reading and editing the AI-generated pseudocode rather than accepting it as-is. Ask \"Does this match your flowchart exactly?\" For Level 5, note responses that confuse loop types to revisit in the next lesson.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Design Loop Flowcharts",
        "progression_name": "For Loop Flowchart",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students stuck choosing a scenario for their for loop, suggest tasks with a known number of repetitions such as completing a set of exercises, reading a set number of pages, or answering a set of questions. For the while loop, suggest scenarios where the outcome is uncertain such as searching for a lost item, waiting for a friend to reply, or playing until someone wins. If students confuse the two loop types, ask \"Do you know exactly how many times this repeats before it starts?\"\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "These levels can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "823ba1ea-0676-4e21-8b42-568f61eebc7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7034b2f8-0a40-401a-8f5f-bf5a80eb7137"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "00fd6d5d-8c5f-4777-aba6-9aa0b13ee82e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answers. Encourage them to address both halves — when we need repetition (when a task involves doing the same steps multiple times) and how we control it (using a for loop when the count is fixed, or a while loop when repetition depends on a condition). Encourage students to use examples from their own flowcharts from Levels 2 and 3, or the free throw scenario from the warm up. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students using the terms iteration, for loop, while loop, count-controlled, and condition-controlled in their responses.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review each vocabulary term."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00fd6d5d-8c5f-4777-aba6-9aa0b13ee82e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2ece659-f893-4eae-8583-086629bd172d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc7b34c4-c03f-4d4b-b9fe-c8aea03f60f9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students physically experience how a for-loop condition controls how many times a loop runs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**Have the class count in unison. After they say 4, pause and emphasize that the loop stops before 5 because the condition is **i < 5**.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>**Circulate:** Watch for students who keep going past 4. Ask them what the condition **i < 5** means and whether 5 is less than 5. Help them connect the math comparison to stopping the loop.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students count again from 0 and let them continue until they say 5. Pause and draw attention to the fact that they said one extra number compared to the first run.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for who stops at 4 versus who keeps going. Ask students to compare **i < 5** and **i <= 5** and explain what changed. Use what you hear to inform how you facilitate the upcoming discussion.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How many numbers did we say in Round 1?*\n- *How many numbers did we say in Round 2?*\n- *What changed in the code?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that changing < to <= adds exactly one more count, and that a single symbol can change the number of loop repetitions by one. Use this to foreshadow that this kind of mistake is called an off-by-one error.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc7b34c4-c03f-4d4b-b9fe-c8aea03f60f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "441468a2-1c9a-4268-8364-9175b0c03673"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24102ffe-546e-4f93-8c96-aa913acb0b69",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify the loop variable and the start, stop, and step values in a for-loop. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students take out their copy of the Iterating with AI Unit Guide.\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "For Loop Anatomy",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Loop Variable"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24102ffe-546e-4f93-8c96-aa913acb0b69",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f61f69a-20c6-419e-b053-ca3981a3e81a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *loop variable*. Point to the i in the code and explain that i is the loop variable — it starts at 0 and changes by one each time the loop runs. Direct students to record the definition and add an example in their Unit Guide.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think the loop variable controls how many times the loop runs, when really it's the condition that does that. If this comes up, acknowledge that i and the condition work together — i changes each time, and the condition checks whether to keep going.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f61f69a-20c6-419e-b053-ca3981a3e81a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa7918ba-7bad-4490-a3e3-e3138ef168b0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to reveal each label one at a time. As each label appears, briefly explain what it represents and direct students to annotate that part of the code in their Unit Guide before moving to the next label.\n* **Start Value:** where the counter begins (i = 0)\n* **Stop Value:** the condition that ends the loop (i <= 10)\n* **Step Value:** how much the counter changes each time (i += 2)\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students often read the stop value as simply \"stop at 10\" rather than \"keep going as long as i is less than or equal to 10.\" If this comes up, ask: \"What is the last value of i that makes this condition true?\" This directly sets up the off-by-one error concept in the next slides.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa7918ba-7bad-4490-a3e3-e3138ef168b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9741b25f-9a6b-43c8-8191-5dec0e61bb87",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students step into the role of a QA team, use the Loop Debugging Checklist to diagnose an off-by-one error in AI-generated code, and understand why AI produces this type of mistake predictably.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce Quality Assurance (QA) teams. Explain that QA teams test software before it ships to catch bugs the original developer missed. Let students know that in the next activity, they take on this role — testing and diagnosing a loop written by AI.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "QA Team Debugging with AI "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9741b25f-9a6b-43c8-8191-5dec0e61bb87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c75e094a-d3df-4cba-83d5-9adc63950dcf",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the Loop Debugging Checklist in their Unit Guide. Explain that before changing any code, QA teams run through each check to diagnose exactly what's wrong. Briefly walk through each check and connect it back to the for-loop parts students just annotated:\n* First Value Test → checks the start value (i = 0)\n* Last Value Test → checks the stop condition (i <= 10)\n* Step Value Check → checks that the counter changes by the right amount (i += 2)\n* Extra/Missing Iteration Checks → catches off-by-one errors at the boundary\n* Match Check → confirms the output actually matches what was asked for\n\nLet students know they use this checklist on the next level before touching any code.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that the checklist is a diagnostic tool, not a fix-it list. The goal is to identify which check the AI failed before touching the code.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c75e094a-d3df-4cba-83d5-9adc63950dcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7ffe3b7-c62c-44e7-9b87-77b779745a44",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1. Remind them they are the QA team testing AI-generated code in a password generator. The AI was prompted to \"write a loop that builds a password with the number of characters the user enters.\" Ask students to run the app multiple times with different password lengths and compare the expected length to the actual length before making any changes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Encourage students to work through the checklist before changing the code. Ask, \"Where does the loop start?\" \"What is the last value the loop should use?\" and \"Does the password length match what the user requested?\" Push students to name which check helped them find the bug.\n",
        "progression_name": "Broken Password Generator",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may want to immediately ask AI to fix the code. Emphasize that the checklist helps them diagnose the problem first. Off-by-one errors are common in AI-generated loops, so this is a place where AI output should be checked carefully rather than trusted automatically.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 1 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objective \"Diagnose and correct off-by-one errors using first, last, and edge tests.\" Look for students who can identify which checklist check the AI-generated loop fails and explain how that connects to the password length being off. Note that the formal vocabulary term is introduced on the next slide — at this point, focus on whether students can diagnose the behavior, not yet name it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7ffe3b7-c62c-44e7-9b87-77b779745a44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3838c715-04a5-43c7-b88c-32848c59054e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define off-by-one error. Connect the definition directly to the password generator students just debugged. Ask students to describe the bug they just found in Level 1. Ask, \"What did you notice about the password length compared to what you requested?\" and \"Which checklist item helped you find it?\" After collecting a few responses, use student observations to help give context and meaning to the term. Point out that the loop condition was i < length - 1 — when a student requested a password of length 8, the loop ran from 0 to 6, producing only seven characters instead of eight. That is an off-by-one error: the loop stopped one iteration too early. Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide and connect it to the example from Level 1.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Off-By-One Error",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think off-by-one errors only happen when mixing up < and <=. Point out that in the password generator, the bug was actually length - 1 in the stop condition — a subtraction that quietly shrinks the count by one. Off-by-one errors can hide in different parts of the loop, which is why the checklist tests each part separately.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3838c715-04a5-43c7-b88c-32848c59054e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c64ee729-654b-42a5-a8d9-2d8b5e7f31ed",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to walk through each step of the loop one at a time. As each step appears, narrate what is happening: the loop checks the condition, and if true, runs and increments i. Draw students' attention to Step 4 — when i = 3, the condition 3 <= 3 is still true, so the loop runs one extra time. Pause here and ask, \"Why does the loop run here when we only expected three runs?\" After collecting responses, advance to Step 5 to show the loop finally terminates when 4 <= 3 is false.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This diagram shows an off-by-one error where the loop runs one time too many. The password generator in Level 1 showed the opposite — the loop stopped one time too early. If students only connect off-by-one errors to one direction, ask: \"Could a loop also stop too early? Where did we see that?\" Use both examples to show that off-by-one errors can go either way, and that the checklist catches both.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c64ee729-654b-42a5-a8d9-2d8b5e7f31ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9cbe2f45-bcb3-4475-b74a-d6a0e813cfcb",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students independently implement a for-loop with correct start, stop, and step values to meet a target output, and demonstrate understanding of off-by-one errors through the CFU level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 2, Levels 2-3.\n* **Level 2:** Students use a for loop to implement a feature in an app.\n* **Level 3:** Students complete a check for understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Read students' AI prompts and push them to be specific: \"From what number to what number?\" \"Should it go up or down?\" \"How much should it change each time?\" Watch their tests and ask, \"Did it stop exactly where you wanted?\" to surface off-by-one errors.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Apply Counted Repetition",
        "progression_name": "Add Counted Repetition",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to iterate: if the AI's first answer is not quite right, have them refine their prompt or manually tweak the loop rather than starting over. If students struggle with adjusting the start, stop, or step values, have them draw a number line and mark the start, each step, and the point where the stop condition becomes false."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to iterate: if the AI's first answer is not quite right, have them refine their prompt or manually tweak the loop rather than starting over. If students struggle with adjusting the start, stop, or step values, have them draw a number line and mark the start, each step, and the point where the stop condition becomes false.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 2 and 3 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objective \"Implement for loops with start, stop, and step values to meet a target output.\" Look for students who can correctly identify the start, stop, and step values needed to produce the target output and explain why an incorrect condition would cause an off-by-one error.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9cbe2f45-bcb3-4475-b74a-d6a0e813cfcb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf2494c4-9561-49e5-8df6-6c3ae493f1d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b17b458e-3dfc-4f9e-a679-ad36a1f0e193",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their response. Encourage them to use specific examples from the lesson — the warm up counting rounds, the password generator bug, or the flow diagram — to explain how the start, stop, and step values in a for-loop control how many times it runs. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, do a quick whip-around and collect one or two responses. Listen for students who can connect the condition (i < 5 vs i <= 5) to the number of repetitions, or who reference the off-by-one error as an example of what happens when the stop value is wrong. These are signals that the core objective landed.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b17b458e-3dfc-4f9e-a679-ad36a1f0e193",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e21b7164-28f0-4a0c-89a7-f273da6e411a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "919862eb-493d-4473-a12f-d10333cba43e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review each vocabulary term and its definition."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "919862eb-493d-4473-a12f-d10333cba43e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e21b7164-28f0-4a0c-89a7-f273da6e411a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d806afa-ab85-445a-8d27-4e526a7e3d83",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *What is the game trying to do here?*\n* *When should it stop? What would have to happen for it to stop?* \n* *Have you ever been stuck waiting on a screen like this?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students recognizing that the game is repeating a search — and that it's supposed to stop when it finds a match. You're not looking for technical language yet. If a student says something like \"it keeps looking but never finds anything\" or \"it should stop when it finds someone to play with,\" that's the thinking you want. The exit-and-reopen loop in the video is a bonus hook — the player is manually doing what the code should have done automatically.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d806afa-ab85-445a-8d27-4e526a7e3d83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "64a56c35-4812-4c29-a490-597319c9a821"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf521bf0-0eb3-4489-a98b-0367b93d2334",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "That search screen is running a loop — keep looking for an opponent until you find one. When the loop works, it stops the moment a match is found. When it doesn't, it just keeps going. Someone has to design that loop and make sure it always knows when to quit. That's a big part of what a Game Engineer does — and today, that's the role you're stepping into.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf521bf0-0eb3-4489-a98b-0367b93d2334",
        "lesson_activity.key": "64a56c35-4812-4c29-a490-597319c9a821"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09745a6a-fc10-4483-91ff-ededff517441",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09745a6a-fc10-4483-91ff-ededff517441",
        "lesson_activity.key": "64a56c35-4812-4c29-a490-597319c9a821"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25b1fac4-aeb8-4b63-8f1d-01b1db40c974",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce the Game Engineer career and establish the through-line that almost every system in a game runs on a loop. This sets up the \"engineer reviewing AI code\" role students take on for the rest of the lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the slide to explain that Game Engineers build the systems that make a game run. Then walk through each example, emphasizing the bolded loop language (while, until, for as long as) — these are the words engineers use when they describe how systems repeat and stop. After the examples, ask: \"What is one game you play (board game, card game, video game), and what is a system in it that probably runs on a loop like this?\" Take two or three quick examples from the class — do not validate or correct, just collect.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "What Does a Game Engineer Do?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may conflate Game Engineers with game designers (who decide what happens) or artists (who decide how it looks). Game Engineers decide how it happens in code — the systems that make the rules actually run.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25b1fac4-aeb8-4b63-8f1d-01b1db40c974",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bdf6d8da-fcea-456e-a9c9-f3031b253e90",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students take out their Unit Guides and turn to Lesson 3. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bdf6d8da-fcea-456e-a9c9-f3031b253e90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d4e4d90-36b8-4f9b-8558-19abcb9cf26f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to interview Rylee, a Game Engineer. Before they start, let students know that Rylee is an AI persona — not a real person with a real job at a studio. Tell them to treat the interview as genuine practice, but to pay attention as they go: does this answer sound like real working knowledge, or does it feel vague and made-up? Plan for about 5-8 minutes here — if most students have asked their three questions and started capturing in their Unit Guide, move on rather than letting the interview run long.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Listen for students asking only the \"fun\" questions (worst bug, coolest game) and prompt them to ask at least one AI-related question, as the instructions require. If a student gets a generic or vague answer, encourage them to push back: \"Can you give me a specific example?\" Watch for students who finish their interview quickly and aren't capturing anything in their Unit Guide — redirect them to the two things learned + one surprise prompt.\n",
        "progression_name": "Talk to a Game Engineer",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a student is stuck on what to ask, point them to the question bank in their Unit Guide — nine pre-written questions organized by Career, Bugs, and AI. They only need to choose three.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d4e4d90-36b8-4f9b-8558-19abcb9cf26f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7f94bca-421e-4a49-8502-4fbeaf2210d3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *Did anyone get a really useful answer about how Game Engineers work with AI?* \n* *Did anyone get an answer that felt off, generic, or maybe wrong?* \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students naming specific things Rylee said, not general impressions. For the second prompt, listen for students who can articulate why an answer felt off — vague language, no specifics. If a student wants to recount a long bad answer, redirect: \"What about it felt off?\" The goal is for students to practice naming what makes an AI response untrustworthy, not to retell the answer for the class.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this share-out to about two minutes — one or two responses per prompt is enough. This is a quick AI literacy beat, not a deep discussion.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7f94bca-421e-4a49-8502-4fbeaf2210d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "090a6b8f-bcee-4b61-b912-d61b486df64f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just practiced evaluating AI as a Game Engineer would. Before you do that work yourselves, let's pick up two words Game Engineers use all the time — and then a checklist they use every time they read a loop.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "090a6b8f-bcee-4b61-b912-d61b486df64f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21ab4b69-c1c5-4a73-be0d-76b2341f8839",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students the two vocabulary terms and the diagnostic checklist that Game Engineers use when working with while loops. Students need both before they can do the engineer's work in the next activity — reviewing AI-generated code and shipping fixes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define state variable using the slide. Then point students to the example code on the right and click the animated slide to ask students: \"If a game has a loop that runs while the player is alive, what is the state variable?\" Give students a moment to think, then take one or two responses. Click the animated slide to reveal that playerAlive is the state variable. Connect it back to the Game Engineer's work: a state variable is how the engineer keeps track of what is happening in the game. Other state variables a Game Engineer might use: bossDefeated, powerUpActive, playerDetected. Each one tracks something the game needs to know to decide what to do next.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Think Like a Game Engineer",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "State variables are usually booleans (true/false) but do not have to be. A timer counting down (powerUpTimer = 10) is also a state variable — it tracks the state of the power-up over time. Worth mentioning briefly so students don't think state variables are only true/false.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21ab4b69-c1c5-4a73-be0d-76b2341f8839",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d260a01-861a-47bc-ad79-07cf3bb677fb",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define infinite loop using the slide. Point students to the example code on the right and tell students: \"A Game Engineer wrote this power-up timer. It should count down from 10 to 0, but it never stops. What is wrong with the loop?\" Give students a moment to think, then take one or two responses. Click the animated slide to highlight the bug — the timer is incrementing instead of decrementing. Connect this to a Game Engineer's job: infinite loops freeze games, break power-ups, and crash matchmaking systems. Engineers run a checklist on every loop to catch bugs like this before they ship.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "There are three common reasons a while loop runs forever, and Game Engineers learn to recognize all three: (1) the condition can never become false (e.g., while (true)), (2) the state variable is never updated inside the loop, (3) the update goes in the wrong direction (this slide's example). Students will encounter cases 2 and 3 in Level 2 — naming the pattern here primes them to recognize it later.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d260a01-861a-47bc-ad79-07cf3bb677fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b917c863-3408-4b84-bfe9-f71d71ad7128",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Game Engineers don't write a while loop and hope it works — they run it through a checklist. Here's the one we'll use as Game Engineers today.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b917c863-3408-4b84-bfe9-f71d71ad7128",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c89c7b2-7017-4f10-bcea-7ac38f33b099",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly walk through both questions on the checklist and have students follow along in their Unit Guides. Use the boss fight example throughout all three checklist slides so students see one loop fully audited end-to-end:\n* **What is this loop trying to do?** Keep enemies appearing until the boss is defeated.\n* **What's the stopping condition?** bossDefeated == true — the loop runs while it's false, and stops when it becomes true. \n\nTell students this checklist is what a Game Engineer runs through every time they write or review a while loop. It's the same checklist they use in the next activity.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Walk through one example loop across all three checklist slides so students see how a single loop gets audited end-to-end. By the third slide, students should be able to apply the same walkthrough themselves in the next activity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c89c7b2-7017-4f10-bcea-7ac38f33b099",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "682506ab-7768-4b11-812d-67158f08152e",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Continue walking through each question on the checklist using the boss fight example from the previous slide:\n* **Start value:** bossDefeated starts as false, so the loop enters and runs. ✓\n* **State variable changes:** No — nothing inside the loop ever changes bossDefeated. ✗\n* **Loop stops:** No — because bossDefeated never changes, the condition never becomes false, and the loop runs forever.\n\nTwo of the three safety checks fail. This is exactly the kind of bug Game Engineers catch with the checklist.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "682506ab-7768-4b11-812d-67158f08152e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8259a17-71bf-4a48-b81d-44f5e690f468",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Finish walking through the checklist using the boss fight example:\n* **What will happen if I run this code as-is?** The enemies would keep appearing, the player could never win. Nothing inside the loop ever flips bossDefeated to true, so the condition never becomes false. The browser eventually freezes or the safety system in the game cuts the loop off.\n\nEnsure students understand that Game Engineers do not just write loops and hope they work — they run loops through this checklist before they send it to players.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8259a17-71bf-4a48-b81d-44f5e690f468",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6b2f90d-21f3-42df-a4cb-284763a4d511",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you know the same checklist Game Engineers use. Time to use it yourself — you're going to review AI-generated game code and use this checklist to find what is wrong.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6b2f90d-21f3-42df-a4cb-284763a4d511",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "015d95df-bfcb-4199-8e71-5e0d2f1ebf60",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 2–3. Students only need to complete one choice in Level 2 — Choices A, B, or C if they want to debug, or Choice D if they want to build. Students who finish early can try the other type: if they debugged, point them to Choice D, and if they built, point them to one of the debug choices.\n* **Level 2:** Students work as Game Engineers and choose to either debug a game's infinite loop (Choices A, B, or C) or build a while loop with AI (Choice D).\n* **Level 3:** Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Help students who are jumping straight to changing code without thinking. Ask them to point at the state variable and the stopping condition before they make any changes. For students working on Choice D, listen for vague AI prompts (\"write me a while loop\") and push them to be specific — what does the loop need to do? What does it need to stop on? Watch for students who accept AI's code without verifying it. Ask them: \"Did you run the checklist on this before you accepted it?\"\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Be The Game Engineer",
        "progression_name": "Be the Game Engineer - Debug an AI's Game Loop",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "While there are safeguards in place to keep infinite loops from crashing a computer, students may encounter one as they work with AI on Choice D. If a browser freezes, use it as a teachable moment: show how an infinite loop ties up the browser tab, then point directly to the condition and update that caused it.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use these levels as formative assessment of the lesson objectives.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "015d95df-bfcb-4199-8e71-5e0d2f1ebf60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c011d32-4a4f-4141-9262-053a73a3af4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67ef34a6-b043-4809-abfd-8854ceeeaa9c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answers. Encourage them to address both halves — how a Game Engineer knows the loop will stop (the condition, the state variable, and how it changes inside the loop) and what would happen if the loop did not stop (frozen game, runaway enemy spawner, expired power-up that never wears off, the safety cap cutting in). Encourage them to use examples from Level 2, Level 3, or the boss-spawner code from the checklist. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67ef34a6-b043-4809-abfd-8854ceeeaa9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fc0c1e2-0f0c-4659-b105-021c6509590b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6afb5ca-8c6e-4091-9cc1-15d950f680b1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students connect their own app experiences to how much information is shown and how clearly it is presented.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which version feels most helpful or realistic?*\n- *Which one feels confusing or overwhelming?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that clear messages and the right amount of information increase trust, while either missing information or information overload can feel confusing or suspicious. Steer the conversation toward the idea that designers and programmers make choices about what to show, what to hide, and how to explain what is happening.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does each version affect your trust that the app is working correctly?*\n- *When is it better to limit the data vs. show it all?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that clear messages and the right amount of information increase trust, while either missing information or information overload can feel confusing or suspicious. Steer the conversation toward the idea that designers and programmers make choices about what to show, what to hide, and how to explain what is happening.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6afb5ca-8c6e-4091-9cc1-15d950f680b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25c1d8bb-4b56-458a-b235-a2484a5eaf1a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6854422-1128-427f-8bcd-be049d2115bf",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Apps constantly decide how much to show and how clearly to show it. When we display data using loops, we are doing the same thing: deciding what users see, deciding what stays hidden, and  explaining when there is nothing to display.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6854422-1128-427f-8bcd-be049d2115bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25c1d8bb-4b56-458a-b235-a2484a5eaf1a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db5ba8cd-51f2-41d0-9741-cf036f21ab4b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db5ba8cd-51f2-41d0-9741-cf036f21ab4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25c1d8bb-4b56-458a-b235-a2484a5eaf1a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0ff95cd-81f0-45e8-8b4b-3e1407561157",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand what a transparency cue is and apply it by adding count messages, filter labels, and empty-state messages to an app.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define transparency cue. Before giving the definition, point to the app mockup and ask students what they notice — specifically what the two arrows are pointing to. After collecting responses, introduce the term and connect it back to Version B from the warm up (\"Showing 3 closest available\"). Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide.\n\n\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Transparency Cues",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Transparency Cue",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think any text in an app counts as a transparency cue. Clarify that a transparency cue specifically communicates something about the data — how many results there are, what filters are active, or why the list is empty. A button label or heading is not a transparency cue; \"Showing 5 of 18 results\" is.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0ff95cd-81f0-45e8-8b4b-3e1407561157",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca73f4c2-fdfe-4269-99de-81ff1e3aca67",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca73f4c2-fdfe-4269-99de-81ff1e3aca67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5971585a-1143-485d-bf91-01d4742376be",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1. Remind them they are now applying the transparency cues they just defined — adding count messages, filter labels, and empty-state messages to make an app clearer and more trustworthy for users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that their count message updates correctly when the dataset changes, that the filter label accurately describes the rule, and that the empty-state message appears only when there are truly no matches. Ask: What is your loop doing each time it runs? How does your message change when there are zero results?\n",
        "progression_name": "Adding Transparency",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to phrase friendly empty-state messages, suggest they write it like they are texting a friend—clear, polite, and specific (for example, \"No showtimes match this time. Try another hour.\").\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5971585a-1143-485d-bf91-01d4742376be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b33a7b6d-260b-4580-a04c-72127f046507",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point students back to the starter code from Level 1 — the for loop was already there while they were adding transparency cues. Before clicking through the annotations, ask: \"What do you think movies[i] is doing each time the loop runs?\" Take a few responses. Then walk through the callouts: i is a counter that starts at 0 and increases by 1 each time, tracking the current position in the array. `movies[i]` uses that position to grab one movie at a time — first movie at 0, second at 1, and so on. The loop keeps going until it's worked through every item. Note that students who worked on the restaurant finder would have seen `restaurants[i]` instead — the name is different but the concept is identical.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students don't need to memorize the syntax here — if they can say \"movies[i] grabs the movie at that position,\" that's enough to move forward.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b33a7b6d-260b-4580-a04c-72127f046507",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7acda786-ea25-481c-9f07-39eae013b20d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Using the same code snippet, shift focus to `movies.length` in the loop header. Ask students where they saw movies.length in Level 1 — they should connect it to the transparency cue they wrote. Students who used the restaurant finder will have written `restaurants.length` instead, but the idea is the same: it's the total number of items in the array, and the loop uses it to know when to stop. Then land the `.map()` connection: in Unit 3, `.map()` was handling all of this automatically. The for loop makes the same process visible — students can now see and control each part themselves.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The `.map()` callout is about recognition, not a deep comparison of the two methods. If students say \"oh, `.map()` was doing this same thing,\" that's the goal.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7acda786-ea25-481c-9f07-39eae013b20d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6772029-8d4e-4514-b009-6a9576d3f1b5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *Which transparency cue in your app makes the biggest difference for users?*  \n* *How do your messages change when the list is empty versus full?*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect their specific count messages, filter labels, and empty states to user trust and clarity. Listen for comments about feeling \"less broken\" or \"more honest\" when the app explains itself. Highlight examples where students carefully matched their messages to what the loop is doing with the data.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6772029-8d4e-4514-b009-6a9576d3f1b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c3ab1d01-ecef-4893-9c68-decbfe4838f8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand the read → transform → render pipeline concept and apply it by building a complete display pipeline for a community events app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define pipeline. Point to the flow diagram on the right and walk through each stage — the document represents raw data being read, the gear represents transforming or reshaping that data, and the chart represents rendering the result for the user. Connect back to Level 1 by asking: \"When you added transparency cues in Level 1, which stage were you in?\" (Render.) Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Build a Display Pipeline"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c3ab1d01-ecef-4893-9c68-decbfe4838f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1b648bb-97af-4a51-8db2-c22451b07d73",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the names of the three stages of the display pipeline. Walk through each stage on the slide and briefly explain what purposeful choices look like at each one: for Read, students decide which dataset their app uses; for Transform, they use loops and conditions to select only what fits their purpose; for Render, they decide which fields to show, how to format them, and what transparency cues to include. Connect back to Level 1 — students were working in the Render stage when they added transparency cues. Now they build the full pipeline from the start.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Motivate the pipeline by connecting it to apps students already use — Netflix deciding what to recommend, Yelp filtering restaurants by distance and price. Let students know they are building that same pattern for their own list view. This helps the three stages feel purposeful rather than arbitrary.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1b648bb-97af-4a51-8db2-c22451b07d73",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac39e82f-4d9f-4ba3-91a4-d154c126f13b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Levels 2-3.\n* **Level 2:** Students build a simple read → transform → render pipeline for community events.\n* **Level 3:** Students answer a check for understanding question\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students correctly looping over the array, applying conditional checks in the loop, and updating the page with only the selected items. Ask: \"Where in your code are you reading from the array?\" \"Where are you deciding which items to keep?\" \"Where are you actually writing to the page?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Build a Display Pipeline",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may combine their transform and render stages inside one loop without realizing it. If this comes up, ask them to point to exactly where in their code they are making a decision about the data versus where they are displaying it. This doesn't need to be corrected as a bug — it's an opportunity to name what each part is doing.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 2 and 3 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives \"Construct a read → transform → render pipeline for an array dataset\" and \"Render selected fields and display a clear empty-state when the array is empty.\" Look for students who can identify which part of their code corresponds to each pipeline stage and whose empty-state message appears correctly when no items match the filter. In Level 3, look for students who can identify how tone, label choice, and hiding information can influence user perception — not just describe what looks different between the three cards. For question 2, look for students who can connect specific parts of their pipeline code to decisions about what gets shown or hidden — this assesses \"Explain how showing counts and empty states supports transparency and trust.\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac39e82f-4d9f-4ba3-91a4-d154c126f13b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c980eb9-0289-404c-8b92-e57cfbfd806c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *Where in your code were you reading from the array?*\n* *Where were you transforming it?*\n* *Where were you rendering it?*\n* *How did your pipeline decide what to show and what to hide?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to point to a specific part of their code and name which pipeline stage it belongs to — not just describe what the app does, but where in the code each stage is happening. Listen for students who can distinguish between where they're making a decision about the data (transform) versus where they're writing it to the page (render). If students combined their transform and render inside one loop, that's fine — use it as an opportunity to name both parts rather than treating it as an error. If time is short, prioritize the first three bullets and use the fourth as a stretch question.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Pipeline"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c980eb9-0289-404c-8b92-e57cfbfd806c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "634182bd-702e-44ab-ab25-68bd511fb26e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93957e73-8648-40db-8dd2-1e15f2727963",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their response. Encourage them to use specific examples from the lesson — the movie showtime app mockups from the warm up, the transparency cues they added in Level 1, or the pipeline they built in Level 2 — to explain how loops move through an array one item at a time and use that process to build what the user sees on screen. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, do a quick whip-around and collect one or two responses. Listen for students who can connect the loop traversal (reading each item in the array one at a time) to the render stage (writing each item to the page). Students who can name both parts of that connection have the core idea. If students answer only in terms of what the app shows rather than how the loop produces it, ask: \"What is the loop actually doing each time it runs?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93957e73-8648-40db-8dd2-1e15f2727963",
        "lesson_activity.key": "94282fae-1bce-483b-8125-817ed0230bb4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aaae1950-1d0b-4cc7-9f9c-1b3491349cd1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review each vocabulary term.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aaae1950-1d0b-4cc7-9f9c-1b3491349cd1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "94282fae-1bce-483b-8125-817ed0230bb4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fb4dd9c8-1750-42c8-aafa-0e808fc435c9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students spot cluttered design and missing data before they start coding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students look at the card and identify the three most important fields with a partner. Then call on a few students to share with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students notice that (1) cards can feel cluttered when every field is shown and (2) some fields are clearly more important than others. Make sure students are identifying fields that are genuinely necessary rather than just the first ones they notice. Ask probing questions like: Which fields would you still understand the card without? and Which fields would be confusing or misleading to leave out?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use student choices as a quick check of their initial understanding of information priority — a key idea they'll apply when designing and coding cards later in the lesson. Look for students who can articulate why a field matters, not just name it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fb4dd9c8-1750-42c8-aafa-0e808fc435c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4373d92d-c567-4859-83f6-4a2a122c3d0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "916346f6-a6ae-4081-ba5d-778c349498a1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "916346f6-a6ae-4081-ba5d-778c349498a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4373d92d-c567-4859-83f6-4a2a122c3d0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b442e53d-07d0-4eef-9890-b9f0ddb9e0c0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Present Card A and Card B to the class. Have students identify each empty field in Card B and brainstorm a fallback idea for each one — for example, \"Email: Unlisted\" or a placeholder image for the missing photo. Have students share their ideas with a partner, then call on a few to share with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students understand that fallbacks should make missing data visible rather than hide it. Listen for ideas that connect to readability and user trust. Ask probing questions like: If this field is missing, what would you want to see as a user? and How could hiding missing information affect whether a user trusts the site?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may default to simply leaving fields blank or removing them entirely. If you notice this, ask them to consider the user's perspective — what does a blank space communicate versus \"Email: Unlisted\"? This sets up the distinction between hiding data and handling it transparently, which students will apply when coding fallbacks later in the lesson.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b442e53d-07d0-4eef-9890-b9f0ddb9e0c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4373d92d-c567-4859-83f6-4a2a122c3d0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db1f3906-725a-428c-8f95-e10289ada102",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db1f3906-725a-428c-8f95-e10289ada102",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4373d92d-c567-4859-83f6-4a2a122c3d0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7742835-ee13-4484-9ca3-1e3857230640",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is important to consider when displaying information on cards?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that cards can become cluttered when too many fields are shown, and that some fields matter more than others. Listen for ideas about choosing the right fields and being honest about what's missing. Students don't need to use precise vocabulary yet — the goal is to surface intuitions they'll formalize later in the lesson.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7742835-ee13-4484-9ca3-1e3857230640",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4373d92d-c567-4859-83f6-4a2a122c3d0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e37da359-aec8-4baf-8ce1-69a26fcda95f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e37da359-aec8-4baf-8ce1-69a26fcda95f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4373d92d-c567-4859-83f6-4a2a122c3d0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22be7047-3300-4440-b12c-f314baabaf1d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience firsthand how the presence or absence of field labels affects how AI reasons about missing data — building the foundation for understanding why labels matter in data design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Levels 1–2.\n* **Level 1:** Students prompt AI to fill in missing values in a dataset with clear field labels.\n* **Level 2:** Students prompt AI to fill in missing values in the same dataset with field names removed, observing how AI's guesses change without label context.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students noticing differences between AI's responses in the two levels. Look for students who can articulate that the labeled dataset produced more accurate or consistent responses. Ask probing questions like: What did AI seem to understand in Level 1 that it struggled with in Level 2? and What do you think changed when the field names were removed?\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Explore Missing Data with AI ",
        "progression_name": "Explore Missing Data with AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22be7047-3300-4440-b12c-f314baabaf1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3fdebc0-9a26-42d2-a63d-42d5a3572439"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3cfc806d-f2b7-441d-81c6-7ed6c075e0e9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\nWhat did you notice about how AI Tutor responded differently between the two datasets?\nWhat role do you think the field labels played in how AI understood the data?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice differences in how AI responded across the two datasets and connect those differences to the presence or absence of field labels. Listen for students recognizing that AI was more accurate and consistent when labels were present. Guide students toward the understanding that labels give AI context — without them, AI has to guess what the data even means before it can guess what's missing. This is the core AI literacy insight of the lesson: context shapes everything about how AI interprets and fills gaps in data.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3cfc806d-f2b7-441d-81c6-7ed6c075e0e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3fdebc0-9a26-42d2-a63d-42d5a3572439"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c76adc8b-629b-465d-8cbd-b9361979f3d6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the term fallback value and walk through the definition and diagram on the slide. Connect the term back to what students just experienced — in the warm up, students came up with ideas for what to show when Card B was missing fields, like \"Email: Unlisted\" or a placeholder image. Those were fallback values, even if students didn't have the name for them yet. In the levels, AI Tutor was essentially doing the same thing — making a guess about what to fill in when data was missing. A fallback value is how we as developers make that decision intentionally and transparently, rather than leaving it to chance or hiding the gap entirely.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c76adc8b-629b-465d-8cbd-b9361979f3d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3fdebc0-9a26-42d2-a63d-42d5a3572439"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0bd0ec9-8615-4d34-ad12-328b1268fa29",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply what they learned about field selection and fallback values by designing a card layout, using AI Tutor to write conditional checks for missing data, and reflecting on how labeling choices can introduce bias or reduce transparency.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5. For Levels 3 and 4, remind students to refer back to their diagrams on Level 1 when deciding which fields belong in the card.\n* **Level 3:** Students map dataset fields onto a card design, deciding which fields to display and planning fallback values for missing data.\n* **Level 4:** Students work with AI Tutor to write conditional checks that apply their planned fallback values in code.\n* **Level 5:** Students complete a Check for Understanding question.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 3, look for designs that prioritize readability and make thoughtful use of field selection and fallbacks. Check that students place field values in the intended card areas and plan specific fallback values rather than leaving gaps undefined. Ask: What should this card show if this field is null? and How could you make that clear to the user? In Level 4, check that students are using AI Tutor's suggestions critically — they should be evaluating whether the conditional checks match their design plan, not just accepting whatever AI generates. In Level 5, encourage students to be specific about what feels biased or unclear and to write transparency labels in plain language a real user would understand.issing data. Ask: What should this card show if rating is null? and How could you make that clear to the user?\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Render Dataset as Cards",
        "progression_name": "Render Dataset as Cards",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look at students' rendered pages to verify that multiple cards appear, fields are in consistent positions, and missing data uses appropriate fallback values rather than breaking the layout. Review students' Level 5 written responses to check whether they can correctly identify what is being highlighted, hidden, or misleadingly labeled, and whether they can explain it clearly — this directly assesses objective 3 (label fields to improve clarity and reduce bias or misinterpretation).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0bd0ec9-8615-4d34-ad12-328b1268fa29",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3fdebc0-9a26-42d2-a63d-42d5a3572439"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9c499cc-70da-4523-9bb7-5f4c6e19c010",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — their card design, a fallback value they chose, or a response from the Level 5 CFU. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting reliable rendering to both the technical choices (fallbacks, conditionals) and the design choices (which fields to show, how to label them) they made today.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and do a quick whole-class share-out instead — collect two or three examples that represent different aspects of the QOTD: one focused on handling missing data technically and one focused on labeling and transparency. This ensures both threads of the lesson get named before students leave.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9c499cc-70da-4523-9bb7-5f4c6e19c010",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24203762-bcc7-4efb-b8d8-5231f335dd50"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3e56499-cd38-4420-99aa-f420822295e4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3e56499-cd38-4420-99aa-f420822295e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24203762-bcc7-4efb-b8d8-5231f335dd50"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3613b200-7592-48cd-afca-e6c6a8493a68",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that filters are decision rules. Even when a filter seems neutral, it can shape who sees opportunities and who is left out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Who would see opportunities with these rules? Who might be left out? What assumptions does this filter make?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the filter favors people who live nearby, have little experience, and are available on weekends. It may leave out people who live farther away, have more experience, or are only available during the week.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3613b200-7592-48cd-afca-e6c6a8493a68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ba4d633-bb8a-41f9-8f81-cc08db49ed2a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29f72533-d8e5-4724-a075-444ba54608a7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn what a Responsible AI Specialist does, then simulate the role by testing OpportunityMatch with user profiles and identifying who is helped, confused, or left out by the app’s current filters.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Introduce the Responsible AI Specialist career.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Responsible AI Audit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29f72533-d8e5-4724-a075-444ba54608a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e530b4e-2afd-4eb8-8d16-5ccfb2381dd8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "oday, you will act as Responsible AI Specialists for an app called OpportunityMatch. OpportunityMatch is a job-finder app that matches young job seekers with local work opportunities based on filters like distance, schedule, and experience level. First, you will test the app using different user profiles. Then you will improve the app so its filters are more inclusive and easier to understand.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students are working with coded filter rules here, not AI. However, Responsible AI Specialists often start by auditing exactly this kind of logic — understanding how rules shape who sees what. In real AI systems, these same questions apply: who gets shown what, and why?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e530b4e-2afd-4eb8-8d16-5ccfb2381dd8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6d2d4a6-ee87-417e-bf03-857c0fa29a32",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6d2d4a6-ee87-417e-bf03-857c0fa29a32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68bdf776-7cf4-4feb-bafe-ea15153cdc4a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:**  Give each student a copy of the Filtering and Transparency activity guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68bdf776-7cf4-4feb-bafe-ea15153cdc4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "166342ae-1a15-4618-a9d7-f26375a294e3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1. Students should complete Levels 1 and 2.\n* **Level 1:** Students learn more about the Responsible AI Specialist role.\n* **Level 2:** Students audit OpportunityMatch using the user profiles and audit checklist.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students testing the app from different user perspectives, not just their own. Ask probing questions like: Which user profile does this filter help? Which user might be confused or left out? What does the app let users control, and what is still missing?\n",
        "progression_name": "Responsible AI Specialist",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students’ audit checklist responses to gauge whether they can connect specific filters to who is helped, confused, or left out by the app.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "166342ae-1a15-4618-a9d7-f26375a294e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67890f0a-c920-4fd6-9b51-92e239571c68",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What did testing the app like a Responsible AI Specialist help you notice that you might have missed as a regular user?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**  Students compare using the app normally with auditing the app critically. They should recognize that a regular user might focus only on whether the app “works” for their own use case, while a Responsible AI Specialist looks for who is helped, who is left out, what filters control the results, and whether the system is transparent.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67890f0a-c920-4fd6-9b51-92e239571c68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "911cf5c2-674d-44ce-b3ad-9bcc22cb6ae5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students improve OpportunityMatch by making the filters more inclusive and adding transparency labels so users understand what is being filtered.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Define active filter.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Improve the Filters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "911cf5c2-674d-44ce-b3ad-9bcc22cb6ae5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65525741-b429-4dcb-90e1-097a9fceba59",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Review compound conditionals as a way to combine more than one filter rule in code. Explain that && means **AND**, so all conditions must be true, while || means **OR**, so at least one condition must be true.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65525741-b429-4dcb-90e1-097a9fceba59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea66f542-1eb9-4767-9f45-82a94d29e4fc",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Active filters often use compound conditionals in the background. For example, an app might only show opportunities that are within 5 miles **and** require 1 year or less experience. That uses && because both conditions must be true. An app might also show opportunities that happen on weekends **or** evenings. That uses || because either condition can be true.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect this review directly to transparency. When users can see the active filters, they can better understand which rules are limiting their results. Point out that && usually narrows the results, while || usually expands them.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea66f542-1eb9-4767-9f45-82a94d29e4fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3fd9938f-4294-42e5-8141-667e9ae35881",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you have audited OpportunityMatch, you will improve it. Your goal is to make the filters more inclusive and help users understand what is being filtered.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3fd9938f-4294-42e5-8141-667e9ae35881",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "517eddab-34b0-424a-90da-2d0f814a6095",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5.\n* **Level 3:** Students improve the app’s filter options.\n* **Level 4:** Students add or revise transparency labels so users understand what is being filtered.\n* **Level 5:** Students reflect on how their changes made OpportunityMatch in Level 4.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Check that students’ filter conditions correctly compare the fields in data.js, such as distance, experience, schedule, pay, or category. Make sure students generated code is using && when they mean both conditions must be true, or using || when they want either condition to be true. Ask: When would this filter return true? Which opportunities disappear because of this rule? Which user profile might be excluded if this condition is too strict?\n",
        "progression_name": "Role Simulation",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students’ updated filter code in Level 3 to gauge whether they can use fields from data.js to create filters that give users more control. Use students’ transparency labels in Level 4 to gauge whether they can clearly explain what a filter is showing and what may be hidden. Use students’ final responses to gauge whether they can connect the Responsible AI Specialist role to concrete actions: auditing filters, improving user control, and increasing transparency.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "517eddab-34b0-424a-90da-2d0f814a6095",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3987b266-9918-46fb-9c16-57cbc1b01b82",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect active filters in OpportunityMatch to the broader idea that unclear or hidden filters can limit what users see and create a filter bubble.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define filter bubble.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Filter Bubble Connection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3987b266-9918-46fb-9c16-57cbc1b01b82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50df60a6-acb1-4e24-9822-b99a53d62ce9",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How could the active filters you coded in the OpportunityMatch create a filter bubble if users do not know they are there?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**  Students connect their filter code to the broader idea that systems can quietly limit what users see. They should recognize that filters for distance, schedule, experience, pay, or category can make the app feel helpful while also hiding useful opportunities from some users.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50df60a6-acb1-4e24-9822-b99a53d62ce9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da1cd5d1-39a7-4821-986d-8b36ca74dc7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "10eb20db-9ef4-4f68-b593-cd0378059ea9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review each vocabulary term and definition, asking students for a quick example for each from today's activities."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10eb20db-9ef4-4f68-b593-cd0378059ea9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6e1d15b6-4fa3-4214-8a52-a8c6b4770240"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65964f8e-d860-40ac-aab9-a4babc6a8d5e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will physically represent an array so they can see what happens when they change originals versus copies of the original.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Pure Functions vs Side Effects activity guide and a small stack of sticky notes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the \"Sticky Note Playlist\" warm-up.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how students should shuffle their playlist.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is there a way to go back to your original song order? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students notice that once they mutate the original arrangement, they cannot easily recover the exact original without having saved it somewhere or recreating it entirely."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65964f8e-d860-40ac-aab9-a4babc6a8d5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fd07ed9-daee-4a7a-bf9f-acd893cde9a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16d4a7d7-f580-4eb2-bdac-a1432f810be5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just saw how easy it is to \"lose\" your original data. When we code, this is called \"mutation\" or a \"side effect.\" Now let’s explore how we can avoid having this happen.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16d4a7d7-f580-4eb2-bdac-a1432f810be5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fd07ed9-daee-4a7a-bf9f-acd893cde9a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "186eeff6-ba7c-48e4-acdc-c1de805daa93",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to return their sticky notes to their initial order in the Original Array. Have them create a second copy of each song on a new post-it and shuffle only the new set into a different order in the Updated Array.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how students should make a copy and then shuffle their new playlist.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "186eeff6-ba7c-48e4-acdc-c1de805daa93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fd07ed9-daee-4a7a-bf9f-acd893cde9a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ca1480b-9a15-4f4d-9669-c6951f586a57",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *How is this shuffle different from the first one? What stayed the same and what changed?*\n* *If you want to edit a photo, what advantages do you get by making a copy of the photo and only editing the copy?* \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**\n Students articulate that the first approach mutated or destroyed the original data, while the second approach kept the original intact and only changed a copy of the data. Help students understand how it is possible to recover from mistakes when the original is preserved. Give the class a shared starting point for understanding why copying or preserving originals matters in software design and how this builds user trust.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ca1480b-9a15-4f4d-9669-c6951f586a57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fd07ed9-daee-4a7a-bf9f-acd893cde9a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cb13ed7-a68c-4723-b577-6aab6abc0521",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we'll explore how these same ideas appear in code. Just like photo apps protect your originals, programmers use pure functions to transform data safely—without breaking what came before.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cb13ed7-a68c-4723-b577-6aab6abc0521",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fd07ed9-daee-4a7a-bf9f-acd893cde9a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4595f57-81c6-4602-a4a3-c72e1757f4f1",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4595f57-81c6-4602-a4a3-c72e1757f4f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fd07ed9-daee-4a7a-bf9f-acd893cde9a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e1e0bf7-f582-459a-9637-e75fcd3f3c77",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students a hands-on experience with pure and mutating methods before vocabulary is introduced, so they can build their own observations about how each method affects the original array.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to investigate Pure Methods and Mutating Methods. Ensure students complete the Method Investigator section of their activity guide. \n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are only clicking buttons without comparing the two arrays. Prompt them to describe out loud what changed, if anything, in the original array. Ask questions like \"What stayed the same?\" \"What is different?\" and \"What happens if you click the same method button again?\" Listen for students noticing that some methods leave the original array unchanged while others modify it — this is the key observation to carry into the next slide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Investigate Methods",
        "progression_name": "Investigate Methods",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to click \"Reset Everything\" after each method before trying the next one. Without resetting, the mutating methods will continue modifying an already-changed array, which makes it harder to see the effect of each method independently. Resetting also reinforces the idea that the original data has a fixed starting state — which connects directly to the concept of preserving originals introduced in the warm up.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e1e0bf7-f582-459a-9637-e75fcd3f3c77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f66bebf-b90b-4f38-8e4b-0b3ca0b67630",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *What did you notice about what happened to the originalData array?* \n* *What do you wonder about why some methods changed it and others didn't?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that some methods left the originalData array unchanged while others modified it directly. Students do not need to explain why yet — the goal is to get their observations on the table before vocabulary is introduced. Listen for students noticing that the teal methods always showed a new result in the newData array while the original stayed the same, and that the orange methods changed the original but showed nothing in the newData array. These observations become the foundation for defining pure functions and side effects on the next slides.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Pure Function",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only noticed surface-level differences such as the colors of the buttons or which array had numbers, prompt them further by asking \"What happened to the originalData array after you clicked a Pure Method?\" and \"What happened after you clicked a Mutating Method?\" This redirects attention to the behavior that matters before moving into vocab.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f66bebf-b90b-4f38-8e4b-0b3ca0b67630",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6934bbcb-1878-4190-a9c2-4ccbcf3387a3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6934bbcb-1878-4190-a9c2-4ccbcf3387a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ebb234f-9648-450d-8ae2-f86c95fa59b0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Build students' understanding of pure functions and side effects by connecting vocabulary and definitions to the method behaviors they observed in the exploration.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define pure function. Connect back to what students just observed in the exploration — the teal Pure Methods (.map(), .filter(), and Loop with a copy) all left the originalData array unchanged and returned a new result. Explain that this is what makes them pure: given the same input, they always produce the same output, and nothing outside the function is changed. Point to the diagram — the input array goes in, a new array comes out, and the original remains intact. Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Making Sense of Methods"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ebb234f-9648-450d-8ae2-f86c95fa59b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8682690a-596b-446f-b465-37b13e589cd4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8682690a-596b-446f-b465-37b13e589cd4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9776532a-f06d-4a91-af1e-57179dae56d0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define side effect. Connect back to the exploration — the orange Mutating Methods (.forEach() and Loop) changed the originalData array directly and returned nothing. Explain that this is a side effect: the function reached outside itself and changed something that existed before it ran. Point to the diagram — unlike a pure function, there is no new array coming out. The original is what gets changed. Emphasize that side effects are not always accidental or wrong — sometimes you intentionally want to update existing data — but they need to be recognized and handled carefully. Direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9776532a-f06d-4a91-af1e-57179dae56d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5af94ad0-4242-47cb-9a43-8cfefb25d676",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use this slide to show students exactly where `.forEach()` causes a side effect in code. Point to line 4 — this is where the mutation happens. Instead of computing a new value and storing it somewhere else, `.forEach()` writes directly back into the original array at each index, overwriting what was there. Point to the `.forEach()` call itself and explain that unlike `.map()`, which students used in Unit 3 to return a new array, `.forEach()` has no return value. Ask students: \"In the exploration on Level 1, what happened to the originalData array when you clicked `.forEach()`?\" Use their observations to connect what they saw visually to what line 4 is doing here. Direct students to annotate this example in their Unit Guide, identifying where the mutation happens and what the return value is.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students often assume `.forEach()` and `.map()` behave the same way since both loop through an array. The key distinction is that `.map()` returns a new array while `.forEach()` returns undefined and only makes sense when the goal is to intentionally change something that already exists. If a student asks when you would actually want `.forEach()`, a good answer is when you want to update the original in place and do not need to preserve it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5af94ad0-4242-47cb-9a43-8cfefb25d676",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f280444-fd20-4d73-93bd-50f849dfaaac",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students apply their understanding of pure functions and side effects to evaluate and refine AI-suggested code changes.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Debugging Methods"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f280444-fd20-4d73-93bd-50f849dfaaac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0de030fa-36ce-4b80-be36-ff8ae6c5e528",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've inherited buggy code from another developer. Your job is to act as a Quality Assurance (QA) engineer who must decide if an AI's suggested code change is safe.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0de030fa-36ce-4b80-be36-ff8ae6c5e528",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9f9cdc3-084c-47f0-b66b-04b03e91a3d6",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 7, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 & 3.\n* **Level 2:** Students work with AI to debug a web app. \n* **Level 3:** Students complete a Check for Understanding question.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students to show you where they see mutation in the original code and how the AI version resolves it. Probe with questions like: What happens when you click Reset Data? Is the original array reassigned or modified?",
        "progression_name": "Buggy Filter Update",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 2 and 3 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9f9cdc3-084c-47f0-b66b-04b03e91a3d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ed25503-5842-415b-9dad-6dd993415780",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ed25503-5842-415b-9dad-6dd993415780",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7bf4c38-865e-470b-b56d-d6593fea5dc2",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you use AI to refactor the code and get rid of any side effects?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Highlight that AI suggestions are not automatically correct and that understanding pure functions and side effects helps students critically evaluate and improve AI-generated code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7bf4c38-865e-470b-b56d-d6593fea5dc2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edda087e-4d09-47a5-b57f-e39ef2c7e402"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1378498e-87e2-4b15-a29d-abb673ab2f1a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain the difference using examples from the warm up, level 1, or the code they just debugged. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, collect just two quick examples: one pure transformation and one side effect. Have students name explicitly whether each is pure or has side effects to reinforce the vocabulary.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1378498e-87e2-4b15-a29d-abb673ab2f1a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c5089e2-0b86-43ac-a749-aae231799a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f869770c-c637-49a8-91c6-11acbfee09f5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f869770c-c637-49a8-91c6-11acbfee09f5",
