    },
    {
      "key": "a491a4f8-d5c2-4887-a8b4-9573b70393a8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a491a4f8-d5c2-4887-a8b4-9573b70393a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c5089e2-0b86-43ac-a749-aae231799a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e597566e-95c5-410b-915c-6daccbe3696a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students understand how Undo and Reset rely on preserving earlier versions of data, and why software must protect originals to allow users to recover from mistakes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Copy, Don't Mutate activity guide and a small stack of sticky notes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the activity where they will be simulating how Undo and Reset work using sticky-note arrays. Have students pick four items, write one per sticky note, and place them in the Original Array on their activity guide under Index 0 through 3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to show how the Original Array should look. Point out that Undo History and Current Array are still empty — nothing has changed yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students copy each value from their Original Array onto new sticky notes and place the copies into the Current Array.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**Click through the animation to demonstrate how to add values to the Current Array. Point out that Original and Current match exactly right now — the Current Array is a copy, not the original itself.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students save a snapshot of their Current Array into the Undo History before making any changes. Then have them swap the order of all four values in the Current Array. Emphasize that the snapshot comes first — that's the whole pattern.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how students update the Undo History and make changes to the Current Array. Point out that the Original Array is still untouched — only the Current Array changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students save a snapshot of their Current Array under Index 1 in the Undo History, then make their second change.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how students update the Undo History and make their second change. Point out that there are now two snapshots saved — one for each change made so far.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students undo their most recent change by peeling off the top snapshot from Undo History and rebuilding their Current Array from it. Ask students: which change did that undo — the first or the second?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how Undo works. Point out that the top snapshot was removed from Undo History and used to rebuild the Current Array — one snapshot remains for another Undo if needed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students reset their array by copying directly from the Original Array. Point out that Reset skips the Undo History entirely — it goes straight back to the original, no matter how many changes were made.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how Reset works. Point out that Undo History is now empty and Current Array matches the Original exactly — Reset doesn't step back through history, it jumps straight to the start.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How did Undo behave when you made two changes?*\n- *What did Reset do differently from Undo?*\n- *Why does Undo require saving snapshots?*\n- *Why doesn’t Reset need any history?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that Undo steps back one change at a time using saved snapshots, while Reset jumps directly back to the original baseline. Listen for students making that distinction — that's the key idea carrying into the lesson. The goal is to help them see how storing versions allows recovery from recent mistakes, and how preserving the original dataset ensures they can always return to a known, trusted state.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e597566e-95c5-410b-915c-6daccbe3696a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "03f24e65-5c7b-4852-bfa7-632c7e20d869"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "423b5e62-10e4-4766-9ff8-c6ca616ff599",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "423b5e62-10e4-4766-9ff8-c6ca616ff599",
        "lesson_activity.key": "03f24e65-5c7b-4852-bfa7-632c7e20d869"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c86dc8c9-ef10-44dd-bd58-50b3649b7546",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Connect students’ real-world experiences with Reset/Undo to the idea of designing clear, user-friendly recovery features.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts. \n* *What design choices made the 'Undo' or 'Reset' button feel helpful?*\n* *What made it feel frustrating?*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface ideas like: losing all work is frustrating, small undo steps feel safer, clear labels matter, and people expect Reset to bring everything back to the original state.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n* **Level 1:** Students complete the Reset and Undo user journey diagram.\n* **Level 2:** Students prompt AI Tutor to create and modify a dataset, then test whether AI can restore the original version.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For Level 1, check that 4a (Reset) shows all filters cleared and all products visible, and that 4b (Undo) shows only the most recent filter removed. Ask: \"What's different between your Reset and Undo screens?\" For Level 2, look for students who got something that looks close to the original — have them compare carefully. Ask: \"Is it exactly the same?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Explore: Reset & Undo ",
        "progression_name": "Reset and Undo User Journey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c86dc8c9-ef10-44dd-bd58-50b3649b7546",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9df8d586-df59-405d-80cf-843359cbdf34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eca091bb-a6ef-486a-acc8-7bd2dd6e4272",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eca091bb-a6ef-486a-acc8-7bd2dd6e4272",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9df8d586-df59-405d-80cf-843359cbdf34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01768d2f-b3de-48a4-b8fd-4875230d33b4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What happened when you asked AI to restore the original dataset? How close did it get?*\n- *Why do you think AI struggled to undo the changes — what would it need to get it exactly right?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that AI has no record of what changed — it can only approximate based on what's still visible in the conversation. This sets up why software needs to explicitly save copies of earlier states to enable true Undo and Reset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What pieces of information need to be saved to enable a \"Reset\" feature?*\n- What pieces of information need to be saved to enable an \"Undo\" feature?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that Reset needs the original unmodified dataset saved somewhere it can't be changed, while Undo needs a record of each previous state in order. Listen for students who treat Reset and Undo as the same thing — that's the key misconception the code annotations address next.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01768d2f-b3de-48a4-b8fd-4875230d33b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9df8d586-df59-405d-80cf-843359cbdf34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ac0ac84-7056-4529-bd03-3a9039415f53",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the copy-not-mutate pattern to code, then apply it to implement Reset and Undo functionality.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide in order to walk students through the process for using `.push()` to enable the Undo flow. Explain that first an empty array is needed. Then the loop visits each item in currentProducts and uses `.push()` to add each item into the new array, creating a copy of the current data before any filtering happens. Next, the copied data is stored inside a stateSnapshot object. Finally, that snapshot is pushed onto historyStack so the app has a saved version to return to when the user clicks Undo.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to show how `.pop()` enables Undo. Explain that `.pop()` removes the most recent snapshot from historyStack and assigns it to lastState. The app then restores currentProducts and activeFilters from that saved copy.\n\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Build: Copy, Don't Mutate"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ac0ac84-7056-4529-bd03-3a9039415f53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9df8d586-df59-405d-80cf-843359cbdf34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43e9adb2-53cb-4e1b-910a-0832b999793f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5.\n- Level 3: Students investigate a broken \"Reset\" button and fix the code to ensure the original, unchanged data is properly copied back to the current view.\n- Level 4: Students build a Undo History Stack by ensuring a copy of the current state is saved to the stack before filtering, and the Undo button pops the last state to restore the product and filter data.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding question.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask, Which array should never be changed? Check that students are not mutating originalProducts. Help them set up a correct loop and assign values properly. Check that students push before filtering, not after. Verify that their copies are real copies, not just references to the same array. Ask, What happens to the stack each time you click Undo? Help students trace through one or two Undo clicks.",
        "progression_name": "Fix the Reset Button",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are stuck, encourage them to use AI Tutor to translate the necessary steps into code. For example, they can use a prompt like: \"I am writing a 'Reset' function for a web app. I need to clear an array called currentProducts and then loop through a non-changing array called originalProducts to copy every item back into currentProducts. For students struggling with stack behavior, have them draw a three-box stack on paper and act out Push/Pop with simple numbers before returning to code.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 3-5 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43e9adb2-53cb-4e1b-910a-0832b999793f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9df8d586-df59-405d-80cf-843359cbdf34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2936cd3e-32ec-45a7-bada-b2b440b48928",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — the sticky-note simulation, the AI literacy level, or the code they wrote. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting copying data to enabling recovery features.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and do a quick whole-class share-out instead — collect two or three responses that hit different parts of the question: one focused on when to copy data and one focused on how users recover it. That way both threads of the lesson get named before students leave.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2936cd3e-32ec-45a7-bada-b2b440b48928",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bbfaf865-a8aa-40c6-97ad-ed54b96f1bc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17274831-f9d9-49c3-a9eb-ae95cda6c72c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate prior knowledge about how app labels and categories shape user experience and begin building toward the idea that filter choices can determine who or what gets seen\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which do you prefer? Which would be easier to use?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the labels, categories, and number of filters available will shape what items they see first and what feels easy to find. Website 1 has more filters which might feel more overwhelming to some users, but might help other users find what they are looking for more easily, especially if they are searching by filters that don’t exist in Website 2, e.g. \"Deals\", \"Rating\", or \"Occasion\". Help students recognize that apps with similar products can feel very different because of labels and filters, which in turn can push users toward different choices.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17274831-f9d9-49c3-a9eb-ae95cda6c72c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f7c7835-b7a8-432a-9006-d8b72dde808d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a75042d9-1dda-44df-a703-e5b29cc626fe",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we have seen how labels change our experience in shopping apps, we are going to explore how filters and thresholds can unintentionally hide certain items or people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a75042d9-1dda-44df-a703-e5b29cc626fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f7c7835-b7a8-432a-9006-d8b72dde808d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a545cd3a-fcc8-4a50-a83a-bfcb36985c39",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a545cd3a-fcc8-4a50-a83a-bfcb36985c39",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f7c7835-b7a8-432a-9006-d8b72dde808d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f8f2eac-549d-4604-909f-4d183da88ad7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore an existing filter on a music streaming platform, observe which artists are surfaced and which are hidden, and begin to recognize how a single threshold can create unequal visibility.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1. Have them complete Levels 1 through 2 and jot down their observations while exploring Level 1 in their activity guide.\n- **Level 1:** Students explore the existing filters on a creative platform dataset, see which artists are surfaced, and record their findings.\n- **Level 2:** Students explain how unclear or non-inclusive labels can affect what users find, miss, or think is meant for them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students struggling to notice the pattern of which artists show up, i.e. those with more likes. Ask: Who shows up? Who is missing or hard to find? What labels or filters might be affecting that? Support students who are stuck navigating the interface by telling them to type into the input field and click \"Show Trending Artists.\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Explore the Filters",
        "progression_name": "Explore the Filters",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student activity guide responses (questions 1–6) to check whether students can identify the filter condition, explain who gets left out, and connect the likes threshold to real-world exclusion — not just describe what disappeared. This assesses Objective 1. Before moving on, quickly scan Level 2 responses to check whether students understand how non-inclusive labels affect what users find or miss. If most responses are surface-level, use the upcoming discussion slide to draw out the deeper connection.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f8f2eac-549d-4604-909f-4d183da88ad7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf2ec327-8204-4611-a029-fd899fe71852",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Threshold"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf2ec327-8204-4611-a029-fd899fe71852",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a74bc46a-c1cd-42c4-a2de-00bb580ee9ec",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slides to display the prompts.\n- *Which artists benefited from a high threshold on likes?*\n- *Which artists were left out?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that artists with high like counts — typically those signed to major labels with marketing budgets — benefited from the threshold, while newer, independent, or niche artists were hidden regardless of their talent or output. If students are only naming specific artists from the platform, push them to generalize: What kind of artist tends to have more likes? Why might that be?\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students haven't been formally introduced to the word \"threshold\" yet — that comes in the next slide. Don't correct students who describe the concept without using the term. This discussion is meant to surface their intuition before the vocab makes it precise.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a74bc46a-c1cd-42c4-a2de-00bb580ee9ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3933195b-3593-4c52-864e-db5beed29db3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *threshold*. Use the diagram to show how the cutoff value divides artists into \"shown\" and \"hidden\" groups. Connect back to what students just observed — the filterTrendingArtists() function used a threshold of 1,000,000 likes, which is why the same artists kept appearing. The diagram's x-axis represents likes; everything to the left of the cutoff was hidden, everything to the right was shown on the homepage.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume that a higher threshold is always \"better\" or more selective in a neutral way. Prompt them to consider who sets the threshold and what assumptions are built into that choice — this sets up the redesign work coming next.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3933195b-3593-4c52-864e-db5beed29db3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0da67438-a6a0-4e31-92b5-4666407167ec",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students plan and implement a redesigned filter that surfaces artists currently hidden by the likes threshold, using pseudocode to specify their logic before coding.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the pseudocode planning section in their activity guide before writing or generating any code in the next level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Check that students have written pseudocode before moving to the level. Look for plans that go beyond just lowering the likes threshold — students should be choosing a different property entirely (genre, label, number of albums, etc.) and should be able to explain who their filter surfaces and why. Ask: Who does your filter highlight? Who does it still leave out? Is that intentional?\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Redesign the Filter",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students jump straight to the level without planning, redirect them back to the activity guide. The planning step is the point — students who skip it tend to make filters that are technically functional but don't address the bias problem they identified.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0da67438-a6a0-4e31-92b5-4666407167ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0783c69c-6cc7-4eae-ba3c-0c7104f41ac3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 and 4.\n- **Level 3:** Students redesign the filter and thresholds for the musical artists homepage to minimize bias.\n- **Level 4:** Students examine a set of entries, analyze how the current \"Top Candidates\" filter behaves, and redesign the filter criteria to feature high-quality entries.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students to explain why they chose certain thresholds or properties. Prompt them: Who appears on your homepage now? How do your changes make the platform fairer or more transparent? Encourage students to consider how their choices affect specific groups — emerging artists, niche genres, self-released musicians. At Level 4, shift focus: What patterns do you see in strong entries? How will your filters avoid hiding them?",
        "progression_name": "Redesign the Filter",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to use clear, specific filter labels rather than a single \"Top\" label — for example, \"Displaying Self-Released and Indie artists\" or \"Soundcloud musicians.\" Transparent labels help users understand what they're seeing and why. If students hit errors, prompt them to check that their property name matches exactly what's in data.js — capitalization and spelling matter.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' redesigned filters at Levels 3 and 4 to assess Objectives 1 and 2 (assess thresholds for potential bias; revise filters to improve inclusivity and transparency). Look for students who can articulate why they chose their criteria — not just that they changed the filter, but who benefits from that change and how the label communicates that to users. Students who only lower the likes threshold without reconsidering the property itself may need a nudge toward a more fundamental redesign.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0783c69c-6cc7-4eae-ba3c-0c7104f41ac3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9718acf0-d9d6-402a-b4dc-8c44d238b3f1",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9718acf0-d9d6-402a-b4dc-8c44d238b3f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b1675ac-e0ff-4e2e-9337-eca2ad0a2ac8",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Share one change you made to the Top Artists or Top Candidates filter. Who is now surfaced because of your change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect their design choices to real impacts on visibility, transparency, and fairness for specific groups — not just say \"I changed the filter.\" Listen for students who can name a stakeholder group that benefits (e.g., \"self-released artists now appear\" or \"entries with fewer followers but strong responses show up\"). If students are only describing technical changes, prompt them: Who couldn't be found before your change? Can they be found now? Use this discussion to bridge back to the Question of the Day before moving to the Wrap Up."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b1675ac-e0ff-4e2e-9337-eca2ad0a2ac8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4813a30d-a4a8-4815-9030-9871344c4dfd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "687add92-d292-414e-b9b8-80eb203fb6f4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — the filter change they made, who is now surfaced because of it, or how they labeled their redesigned filter. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting their design choices to visibility and fairness for specific groups — not just describing what they changed technically.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student reflections to check whether they can identify biased filters and propose concrete design changes that improve visibility and transparency."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "687add92-d292-414e-b9b8-80eb203fb6f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "656e95e0-7e97-4198-b916-96e218c4890f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d19e579c-2414-4c7b-8765-792d6870c132",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d19e579c-2414-4c7b-8765-792d6870c132",
        "lesson_activity.key": "656e95e0-7e97-4198-b916-96e218c4890f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02628a8e-7fa5-48b9-a755-f7097351f200",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02628a8e-7fa5-48b9-a755-f7097351f200",
        "lesson_activity.key": "656e95e0-7e97-4198-b916-96e218c4890f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "382ec9c5-ec52-4a20-b886-27e02869ea93",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Engage students with how vague field names can lead to very different interpretations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the form example and briefly introduce that two people and an AI disagree about what some fields mean.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which field names caused the biggest misunderstanding? Why?*\n- *How can small wording choices shape how AI interprets meaning?*\n- *Why might designing clear schemas be an ethical responsibility when AI uses your data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that designing clear, unambiguous data structures is an ethical responsibility — vague field names and unclear types cause misinterpretation, and no person or AI is inherently wrong when that happens. The problem is in the structure itself. Students don't need the word \"schema\" yet — they just need to feel the weight of the design decision before they get the vocabulary for it in the activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the role of a Database Architect role."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "382ec9c5-ec52-4a20-b886-27e02869ea93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abca702e-ee70-410a-89c3-183ea6b04856"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e1a3f6d-6fdc-4115-8ad9-efaa0ac961c3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As we work today, you act like data architects — making choices about field names and data types that shape how both people and AI understand your data.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e1a3f6d-6fdc-4115-8ad9-efaa0ac961c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abca702e-ee70-410a-89c3-183ea6b04856"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1fb57f39-b2f7-4c55-a264-1659295e71bc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1fb57f39-b2f7-4c55-a264-1659295e71bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abca702e-ee70-410a-89c3-183ea6b04856"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "402101b6-a92e-45be-a674-e55588b79a18",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students investigate how unclear field names and types confuse AI and begin revising a schema to improve reasoning.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Misleading Schema"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "402101b6-a92e-45be-a674-e55588b79a18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1273069-1066-4044-a3ab-d7f6348fbae9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine the AI is doing the best it can, but our data labels are sending mixed messages. Today we debug the data, not just the code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1273069-1066-4044-a3ab-d7f6348fbae9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "010f484a-c7e7-4ee2-b2e9-8d091572fbf1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to investigate inconsistent field names and data types in a messy dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students identifying issues like vague labels (\"stars\" vs. \"rating\"), mixed types (\"3\" as a string vs. 3 as a number), or missing values. Ask probing questions like: What do you want this field to mean? and How could you rename it to make that obvious to both a person and an AI? Encourage students to test their changes by rerunning the AI check.",
        "progression_name": "Investigate Misleading Fields",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, suggest they write one clear sentence describing what a field is supposed to represent, then turn that sentence into a better field name and data type.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "010f484a-c7e7-4ee2-b2e9-8d091572fbf1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b0f0127-71fa-42dc-a23d-a30747778d84",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b0f0127-71fa-42dc-a23d-a30747778d84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb168127-2965-43e9-8e46-b45e6357238b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did changing field names or types change what the AI concluded about the data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect specific schema changes — like renaming \"stars\" to \"rating_1_to_5\" or changing a string \"3\" to a number 3 — to changes in AI behavior. Listen for students recognizing that AI isn't guessing randomly — it's reasoning from whatever context the field names and types provide. The clearer and more precise the structure, the more accurately AI can interpret the data. Guide students toward the idea that these structural decisions are design choices with real consequences, which sets up the schema vocabulary introduction that follows."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb168127-2965-43e9-8e46-b45e6357238b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d9218dd2-8c2b-4505-8ffb-b6142f85ba35",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the term schema. Ask students: \"What have we been calling the plan for how our data is organized?\" Let students surface \"data plan\" or similar language, then bridge to the formal term. Connect back explicitly to both the previous and current unit experiences, when students designed the structure of their data — deciding what fields to include, what types they should be, and what rules they should follow — they have been doing schema design. They just didn't have the word for it yet. A schema is the formal name for that plan. ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think a schema is only relevant for large or complex databases. Clarify that any time you make decisions about what fields to include, what type a value should be, or what happens when data is missing, you are designing a schema — even for a small dataset like the ones they've been working with all unit.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d9218dd2-8c2b-4505-8ffb-b6142f85ba35",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5b9a06f-313f-424c-bff3-e0539c1b5aff",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5b9a06f-313f-424c-bff3-e0539c1b5aff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6db3a662-ebd3-4f21-894c-b51517b95c77",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you were a QA tester, what edge cases would you test to make sure the schema really works?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to name specific edge cases — empty values, missing fields, extreme numbers, unexpected data types — and explain why each one could break a feature or produce misleading results. Listen for students connecting edge cases back to the schema decisions they just discussed: a well-designed schema anticipates these cases rather than leaving them to chance. Guide students toward understanding that testing edge cases isn't separate from schema design — it's how you find out whether your schema is actually robust. This sets up the Data Architect Challenge where students will write code to handle exactly these kinds of cases.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6db3a662-ebd3-4f21-894c-b51517b95c77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02794416-611f-410e-816c-f379eb5d8375",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of schema design by diagnosing and cleaning a messy dataset, writing constraint checks to handle edge cases, and reflecting on how their decisions improve both AI reasoning and app reliability.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Data Architect Challenge"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02794416-611f-410e-816c-f379eb5d8375",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "088844a5-acdc-4ed3-b93a-e32c7cdf46a2",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You have seen how vague field names and inconsistent types send mixed messages to AI. Now you design and clean a schema so your app's features work reliably — and test it against the cases most likely to break it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "088844a5-acdc-4ed3-b93a-e32c7cdf46a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "597c4c45-8a86-4c10-b5fc-69ad35db9e48",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 10, Levels 2-3. \n- **Level 2:** Students diagnose and clean a schema so the app's features work reliably, then add constraint checks and run edge cases.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete a Check for Understanding question.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students write loops that touch every object and actually modify keys and values — not just log them. Ask: What fields does every object need? and What type should this field be? As students move into constraint checks, look for clear conditionals that enforce rules and for students who actually try unusual inputs. Ask: What happens if this field is missing entirely? and How would your AI assistant react to this case?",
        "progression_name": "Data Architect Challenge",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage pairs to talk through one example object out loud — what it looks like before and after cleaning — before they generalize to a loop. This helps students catch type issues and missing fields they might otherwise overlook when writing code directly.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' cleaned data output from Level 2 and their written justifications from Level 3 as a formative assessment of all three lesson objectives. In Level 2, look for loops that correctly normalize field names, fix data types, and enforce constraints. In Level 3, look for students who can explain both the original schema problem and why their fix makes the data clearer for AI and more reliable for the app — this directly assesses \"Analyze how field names and types affect AI reasoning and revise to reduce misinterpretation.\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "597c4c45-8a86-4c10-b5fc-69ad35db9e48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4cbf5115-26e2-4f35-ae01-750198f3ac81",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4cbf5115-26e2-4f35-ae01-750198f3ac81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d716f6b-9804-4cd4-802f-7c48f383793c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students turn and talk for one minute. Each partner shares one schema change they made and explains how it makes their feature simpler, more correct, or more fair. Listen for students who can articulate the why behind their decision, not just describe what they changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students who name specific field names, data types, or constraints. If students describe what they changed without explaining the impact, nudge them with: How does that change affect what a user sees or experiences? or What could go wrong if you hadn't made that fix?\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Reflection: Justify Your Design Choice",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' written justifications as a key formative check that they understand how schema decisions affect AI reasoning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d716f6b-9804-4cd4-802f-7c48f383793c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "986f7b9e-16c1-43fd-a4d8-4b80efc8c0ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aaa70ac4-99ab-4c8a-a5e3-e3ec9e5cf953",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — a field they renamed, a data type they fixed, a constraint they added, or a response from their Level 3 CFU. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students who can connect their specific schema decisions to all three parts of the question — simpler, correct, and fair — rather than addressing only one.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and do a quick whole-class share-out instead. Aim to collect one example for each of the three qualities in the QOTD — one that made something simpler, one that made something more correct, and one that made something more fair. This ensures all three threads of the lesson get named before students leave.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aaa70ac4-99ab-4c8a-a5e3-e3ec9e5cf953",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ff65e79e-cc18-45c0-ae13-b0b35bc879b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adc6d758-95e0-4605-b882-b3df5d31f833",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adc6d758-95e0-4605-b882-b3df5d31f833",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ff65e79e-cc18-45c0-ae13-b0b35bc879b1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78ae215a-3b79-4c9e-ad59-2a9baa9dca90",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\nWhen you're trying to find information in an app or website, what makes it easy or hard to explore the data?\nWhat is one thing you wish more apps did to help you find what you're looking for?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on real experiences with findability, data clarity, and user control, setting up the project's focus on schema design, transparency, and meaningful filtering.\n\n\n\n\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78ae215a-3b79-4c9e-ad59-2a9baa9dca90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf5c3323-60c6-4fa4-ad3d-dbf36d68a6cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dee752cb-6a4e-486e-8a9f-33d1647be676",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through these simplified project goals. These summarize what their dashboard must do: display data clearly, support user control through filters and sorting, and communicate choices transparently. Emphasize that fairness and visibility—like which items get surfaced or hidden—will be part of their design thinking throughout the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Interactive Data Dashboard Project Rubric\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program. Listen for students noticing themes like transparency, fairness, active filter displays, loop-driven rendering, and Reset/Undo—these are central to the project and will guide their design decisions.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Interactive Data Dashboard Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from choosing and refining their dataset to building and showcasing their interactive dashboard.\n\n\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dee752cb-6a4e-486e-8a9f-33d1647be676",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf5c3323-60c6-4fa4-ad3d-dbf36d68a6cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa9b3a32-baa2-40dd-9f99-45b24e1cff9a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today's focus is Milestone 1: Choose a Dataset & Define Your Schema, where students select a dataset, narrow it to meaningful fields, and examine it for bias, clarity, and findability. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain Deliverables for Milestone 1 so students understand what needs to be completed by the end of this milestone.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to mention parts of the deliverables that feel most exciting or challenging. This helps you gauge engagement and plan support for different groups. Remind students that Milestone 1 focuses on data decisions — not code. Their goal is to choose a dataset, define a schema, and think about visibility, fairness, and which fields users will filter by.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa9b3a32-baa2-40dd-9f99-45b24e1cff9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf5c3323-60c6-4fa4-ad3d-dbf36d68a6cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c4d09b4-1ba2-44a3-8e6a-02d4b00c6ff8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Level 1 to complete Levels 1–3.\n* **Level 1:** Students choose a dataset, complete `planning.md`, rename it `dataset.js`, and add both files to their Backpack.\n* **Level 2:** Students define their schema, card view, and filter clarity notes in SketchLab. They may use the chatbot to plan or review their choices.\n* **Level 3:** Students consult AI, revise their dataset fields, and update `dataset.js`.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the Milestone 1 level progression, ask guiding questions such as:\n* Which dataset does your project focus on?\n* What fields will be included in your filters and why?\n* Are there any missing groups or unclear categories in your filters or labels?\n* Who might not be visible due to your filter choices right now?\n\nUse these check-ins to confirm students are moving from idea to planning, rather than jumping straight into code.\n",
        "name": "Milestone 1",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing — Milestone 1 can typically be finished by the end of today's class or early next class. Encourage students to focus first on clarity of idea rather than polish.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c4d09b4-1ba2-44a3-8e6a-02d4b00c6ff8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf5c3323-60c6-4fa4-ad3d-dbf36d68a6cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e1746ca-cf46-4ea8-a05f-109a564d6170",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 4 to complete the Milestone 1 Reflection. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are discussing and sharing these reflection questions with a thought partner. \n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Teaching Tip\nIf time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e1746ca-cf46-4ea8-a05f-109a564d6170",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cf5c3323-60c6-4fa4-ad3d-dbf36d68a6cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8151652-4a81-42c4-92ea-7b4e79e9e56c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\nWhen you look at a page full of information (menus, playlists, search results, etc.), what helps you understand it quickly?\nWhat kinds of layouts or labels make information easier—or harder—to scan?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface real-world examples of clear labeling, visual grouping, and layout logic, preparing them for dashboard wireframing and schema → UI decisions.\n\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8151652-4a81-42c4-92ea-7b4e79e9e56c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8daeb373-4028-4b81-a591-56b81b7ded31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47f6bdc7-b6bb-4d06-9880-64251fa938e7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today students will focus on completing Milestone 1 and starting work on Milestone 2. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain Deliverables for Milestone 2 so students understand what needs to be completed by the end of this milestone.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47f6bdc7-b6bb-4d06-9880-64251fa938e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8daeb373-4028-4b81-a591-56b81b7ded31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0768ab9-b6b5-4936-8996-ec50798c590d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Remind students to return to earlier levels if they still need to complete any Milestone 1 work. Then, direct students to Lesson 11, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 and 6 where they will shift from exploring and refining a dataset and outlining their schema, to planning and building their dashboard. Explain that today’s focus is creating clear wireframes and building their static UI that displays all items using loop-driven rendering. Emphasize that students should aim for clarity rather than polish—simple layouts that show where cards will go and how each schema field will be labeled. Encourage students to begin with one clean card layout and build from there, mirroring how developers prototype simple, testable pieces before adding complexity.\n* **Level 5:** Students create the main dashboard wireframe in App View, including filters, sort controls, cards, field annotations, and transparent labels.\n* **Level 6:** Students build the static HTML/CSS page, connect script.js and dataset.js, and use a loop to render all data cards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a complete first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n* Which schema fields will appear on your card and where will they go?\n* Where will users look first when they open your dashboard?\n* Does your wireframe show clear labels for each field and for any future filters?\n* How will your layout make the data easy to scan and compare (e.g., grouping, order, or emphasis)?\n* Where will transparency cues like “Showing X results” or active filter labels appear?\n\nRemind students that their wireframes should clearly map schema fields to card UI elements and show where labels and transparency cues (like “Showing X results”) will go. Provide quick support if students get stuck — redirect them to the Filter & Threshold Planner Bot or Project Support Bot for troubleshooting help before intervening directly. \n\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0768ab9-b6b5-4936-8996-ec50798c590d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8daeb373-4028-4b81-a591-56b81b7ded31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b195e13-f770-4220-9ae0-d22dd4cd6c88",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 7 to complete the Milestone 2 Reflection. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write and discuss, listen for reflections that describe how their page layout and card design help (or fail to help) users scan, compare, and find items quickly and clearly. Encourage students to notice whether their labels, headings, and visual structure make the dataset easy to scan, and to capture specific ideas about what they might refine next time.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b195e13-f770-4220-9ae0-d22dd4cd6c88",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8daeb373-4028-4b81-a591-56b81b7ded31"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2744b3d0-678d-4d1b-914d-5ab89735036e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Looking at your dashboard so far, what's one thing you want users to be able to find easily — and what’s one thing that might still be hard to find?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how their current dashboard layout supports or challenges visibility and findability. This prepares them to design filters, sorting, and transparency features in ways that help users explore the dataset meaningfully.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2744b3d0-678d-4d1b-914d-5ab89735036e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c30e88df-a4f2-422d-b865-d370bd43bd8e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3765ae3f-497d-4b19-aa0f-76fe521e6dbd",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 2 and 3 and read the goal for Milestone 3. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the deliverable to complete for Milestone 3. \n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that these milestones move from shaping how data is displayed to making that data interactive. Encourage students not to aim for perfection at first — focus on getting one feature or transformation working clearly, and then improve it through testing, debugging, and fairness checks.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3765ae3f-497d-4b19-aa0f-76fe521e6dbd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c30e88df-a4f2-422d-b865-d370bd43bd8e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97c31b02-fcf1-4e39-beb1-bcc1f1e413da",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3. Encourage students to TAG areas like filter accuracy, sort behavior, active label updates, and Reset/Undo function. This helps them gather early insights before debugging more deeply.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\n\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance. \n\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97c31b02-fcf1-4e39-beb1-bcc1f1e413da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c30e88df-a4f2-422d-b865-d370bd43bd8e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df9b79ee-5bd1-4d83-8e2f-d5b050fb87d0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 8 to complete Levels 8 and 9.\n* **Level 8:** Students plan the filter logic with a flowchart showing data flow, combined filters, and Reset/Undo behavior.\n* **Level 9:** Students build filters, sorting, Reset, Undo, active filter display, and result count, then test and debug with AI support.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are moving from planning their logic to building and testing interactive features. Ask check-in questions like:\n* What should happen to the original dataset when a filter is applied?\n* Does your flowchart show how filtered copies move from the dataset to the display?\n* How do your filters combine: does the user need to match all filters or just one?\n* What should Reset restore? What should Undo remove or restore? \n* Where will users see which filters are active and how many results are showing?\n* What test case could break your filter or sort logic?\n\nRemind students to use copy-not-mutate strategies so the original dataset stays unchanged. As students code, look for working filters first, then support them in adding sorting, Reset, Undo, active filter labels, and result counts. Encourage students to test combinations, missing values, and zero-result cases before revising their logic.\n\n",
        "name": "Milestone 3",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df9b79ee-5bd1-4d83-8e2f-d5b050fb87d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c30e88df-a4f2-422d-b865-d370bd43bd8e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47876617-fac6-4288-b106-7faedf5aa14a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 10 to complete the Milestone 3 Reflection. Let students know they can continue to add on to this reflection as they continue to make progress on Milestone 3 and conduct more testing.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share how their filter or sort choices influence which items appear first — this helps reinforce fairness and visibility thinking from the rubric. Highlight that catching fairness issues or algorithmic quirks early is a sign of strong computational thinking — developers constantly refine how data is displayed to ensure experiences are both accurate and equitable.\n\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47876617-fac6-4288-b106-7faedf5aa14a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c30e88df-a4f2-422d-b865-d370bd43bd8e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "065ee914-b671-4d78-a311-df98045eea52",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "065ee914-b671-4d78-a311-df98045eea52",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c30e88df-a4f2-422d-b865-d370bd43bd8e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c0f4138-e302-48a2-8e2c-24c779666f83",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What's one part of your dashboard that you think might break or behave unexpectedly if someone tried an unusual filter or sorting choice? Why that part?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students anticipate potential weaknesses or edge cases in their current filtering and sorting logic, preparing them for systematic debugging and refinement in Milestone 4.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c0f4138-e302-48a2-8e2c-24c779666f83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "477988df-8aaf-4d6c-9eb3-7166b07cb06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2e8ff1d4-7130-4bbb-b77f-c6ea9f3d0032",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Goals for Day 4 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 3 and 4. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the deliverables to complete for Milestone 4. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3. Encourage students to TAG areas like: filter accuracy, sort behavior, active filter labels, Rest/Undo, or fairness issues. \n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\n\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\n\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2e8ff1d4-7130-4bbb-b77f-c6ea9f3d0032",
        "lesson_activity.key": "477988df-8aaf-4d6c-9eb3-7166b07cb06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "060bff29-f5d0-490a-9d15-f4a26bbfbf6d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to complete Lesson 11, Level 11 in order to identify edge cases, fix at least two issues, and polish their dashboard for clarity, fairness, and transparency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are testing their app as a whole, not just fixing isolated pieces. Ask check-in questions like:\n* What edge case did AI help you find?\n* What happens if a value is missing?\n* What message appears when no results match?\n* Do any filter combinations feel confusing or misleading?\n* Are your labels, thresholds, and result counts clear to users?\n* Do Reset, Undo, filters, sorting, labels, and result count all work together?\n* Where did you add comments to explain your code?\n\nRemind students to fix at least two issues using conditional logic, then make final improvements to layout, labels, comments, fairness, and transparency. Encourage them to test the full user flow one last time before moving on.\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "060bff29-f5d0-490a-9d15-f4a26bbfbf6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "477988df-8aaf-4d6c-9eb3-7166b07cb06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fec2358c-b174-4bb6-be9d-bf0ca90be9f6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students back to Lesson 11, Level 10 to revisit the Milestone 3 Reflection and add on to what they wrote the previous class period. \n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When students revisit their reflections today, point out that adding new insights after another round of testing is exactly how professional engineers document progress. Encourage them to notice what changed, what improved, and what new questions emerged—this shows they’re thinking like real developers who learn from every test cycle to make smarter, more informed decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fec2358c-b174-4bb6-be9d-bf0ca90be9f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "477988df-8aaf-4d6c-9eb3-7166b07cb06d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d228b7b0-9c65-456c-83c3-35f62e414050",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What's one edge case, label, or detail you want to check today to make sure your dashboard is clear, fair, and ready to share?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify final improvement opportunities tied to clarity, transparency, fairness, or reliability, setting a concrete intention for their last work session.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d228b7b0-9c65-456c-83c3-35f62e414050",
        "lesson_activity.key": "082613f8-13c5-4c65-a9f1-0f1d4d63e4a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ee07a9c-8ea3-4c05-bd26-e79912066e92",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 5 of the project. Explain that today is focused on Milestone 4: Handle Edge Cases & Finalize Your Dashboard, where students test their dashboard with tricky cases, polish their UI and logic, refine for clarity and inclusivity, and make it ready for showcasing. \n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Clarify that students should test unusual filter combinations, missing values, empty result states, and sorting after filtering. These edge cases help ensure the dashboard behaves predictably, transparently, and fairly. Encourage students to check that transparency elements — such as “Showing X results” and active filter labels — always match the state of the data. Remind students that even small adjustments to labels, spacing, or ordering can make a big difference for user clarity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ee07a9c-8ea3-4c05-bd26-e79912066e92",
        "lesson_activity.key": "082613f8-13c5-4c65-a9f1-0f1d4d63e4a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f5cfadb-6688-465e-985d-52e79ba54256",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the final deliverables for Milestone 4. \n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the last two pages of their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide, where the project rubric is located. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion. Encourage students to examine the Ethical Reasoning: Transparency & Fairness row carefully — this project’s dashboard focuses heavily on ensuring filters do not unintentionally hide important items or groups. Under Algorithmic Features, remind students to check whether their Reset/Undo is reliable and whether active filter counts update correctly.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\n\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f5cfadb-6688-465e-985d-52e79ba54256",
        "lesson_activity.key": "082613f8-13c5-4c65-a9f1-0f1d4d63e4a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a44b5f7c-43ea-47b1-9a18-a1fb794ec53a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students back to Level 11 to finish developing their Interactive Data Dashboard Projects. Explain that this is their final dedicated work session for Milestone 4: Handle Edge Cases & Finalize Your Dashboard. Reinforce that today's goal is to prioritize improvements based on their self-assessment and TAG peer feedback, rather than trying to fix everything at once. Instruct students to use the feedback they received from their TAG review and self-assessment to finalize their Interactive Data Dashboard Project. Have students submit their projects at the end of the allotted time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence. \n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\" If time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a44b5f7c-43ea-47b1-9a18-a1fb794ec53a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "082613f8-13c5-4c65-a9f1-0f1d4d63e4a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43ffe138-aefe-4ea2-bf7b-3c4d2524a4f5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Interactive Data Dashboard Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage feedback on clarity, fairness, and transparency. For example:\n* Is it clear what filters are active?\n* Are any items consistently hidden or hard to surface?\n* Does Reset/Undo behave predictably?\n* Is the dataset easy to scan visually?\n* Do labels accurately reflect the data shown?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\" \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43ffe138-aefe-4ea2-bf7b-3c4d2524a4f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "082613f8-13c5-4c65-a9f1-0f1d4d63e4a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7922fea9-fe56-495a-89ca-c36a5d43a99a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've gone from choosing a dataset and defining a schema to building, testing, and refining your Interactive Data Dashboard. This final reflection is a chance to capture how your dashboard evolved, how your filters, labels, and Reset/Undo choices support fairness and user control, and how AI supported your planning, coding, or debugging.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7922fea9-fe56-495a-89ca-c36a5d43a99a",
